OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026", "title": "The ordinary-double-point gap in every dimension", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "037", "family_title": "The ordinary-double-point volume gap", "abstract_html": "We prove the ordinary-double-point gap conjecture for boundary-zero complex algebraic klt germs in every dimension: a singular <i>n</i>-dimensional germ has normalized volume at most \\(2(n-1)^n\\), with equality precisely at an analytic ordinary double point.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "The ordinary double point gap", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\\leq 2(n-1)^n. \\) Equality holds if and only if \\( (X,x)^{\\mathrm{an}}\\simeq \\left(\\{z_0^2+\\cdots+z_n^2=0\\}\\subset\\mathbb C^{n+1},0\\right). \\) No isolatedness, complete-intersection …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All varieties are over \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq 2\\), and let \\(x\\) be a singular closed point of a normal complex algebraic \\(n\\)-fold \\(X\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that \\(K_X\\) is \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier and that \\(X\\) is klt near \\(x\\), with zero boundary.</p><p class=\"li\">For a real valuation \\(v\\) of \\(K(X)\\), trivial on \\(\\mathbb C\\) and centered exactly at \\(x\\), set\n  \\[\n  \\mathfrak a_t(v)=\\{f\\in\\mathcal O_{X,x}:v(f)\\geq t\\},\n  \\qquad\n  \\operatorname{vol}(v)\n  =\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}\n  \\frac{n!\\operatorname{length}(\\mathcal O_{X,x}/\\mathfrak a_t(v))}{t^n}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Log discrepancy is normalized by\n  \\[\n  A_X(E)=1+\\operatorname{coeff}_E(K_{X'}-\\mu^*K_X)\n  \\]\n  for a prime divisor \\(E\\) on a smooth proper birational model \\(\\mu:X'\\to X\\). For real valuations, use its extension by monomial retractions.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}_X(v)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  A_X(v)^n\\operatorname{vol}(v),&amp;A_X(v)&lt;+\\infty,\\\\\n  +\\infty,&amp;A_X(v)=+\\infty,\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\qquad\n  \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\n  =\\inf_{v\\text{ centered at }x}\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}_X(v).\n  \\]\n  The volume limit exists by [9, Theorem 5.8]; monotonicity extends the integer-cutoff limit to real cutoffs. The normalized-volume infimum is unchanged if restricted to positive multiples of divisorial valuations [29, Theorem 2.5].</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\\leq 2(n-1)^n.\n\\]\nEquality holds if and only if\n\\[\n(X,x)^{\\mathrm{an}}\\simeq\n\\left(\\{z_0^2+\\cdots+z_n^2=0\\}\\subset\\mathbb C^{n+1},0\\right).\n\\]\nNo isolatedness, complete-intersection, quotient, or smoothability assumption is imposed.</p>", "proof": "<p>In dimension two, the quotient classification gives an analytic germ\n\\((\\mathbb A^2/G,0)\\), with \\(G\\) finite effective and without reflections. Completion invariance and the finite degree formula give\n\\[\nd(0,\\mathbb A^2/G)=1/|G|.\n\\]\nFor a singular germ, \\(|G|\\geq2\\). Equality with \\(p_2=1/2\\) forces an order-two action without a fixed hyperplane, namely \\((-1,-1)\\), which is the \\(A_1\\) surface singularity.</p><p>The threefold case is [36, Theorem 1.3(1)], and the fourfold case is original Theorem 2.4, the companion input stated in Proposition 2.</p><p>Assume the theorem in all smaller dimensions and let \\(n=N+1\\geq5\\). Proposition 18 proves \\(M_n\\leq p_n\\), giving the required bound for every singular germ.</p><p>If \\(d(x,X)=p_n\\), Proposition 2 gives a singular K-polystable cone \\(C\\) of the same density and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{edim}(X,x)\\leq\\operatorname{edim}(C,o).\n\\]\nProposition 19 makes \\(C\\) an ordinary double point, so\n\\(\\operatorname{edim}(X,x)\\leq n+1\\). Since \\(x\\) is singular, its embedding dimension is exactly \\(n+1\\).</p><p>It is a hypersurface germ: start with an embedding in a smooth ambient variety and use equations with independent linear parts to cut the ambient germ down to dimension \\(n+1\\). The remaining ideal is a height-one prime in a regular local ring, hence principal. Proposition 20 makes its quadratic part nondegenerate, so its analytic germ is ODP.</p><p>Conversely, Proposition 1 gives the stated normalized volume at every analytic ordinary double point. This completes both the equality characterization and the induction.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Global volume bounds", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\(V\\) is singular, then \\( (-K_V)^n\\leq 2\\left(\\frac{n^2-1}{n}\\right)^n<2n^n. \\) If \\(V\\not\\simeq\\mathbb P^n\\), then \\( (-K_V)^n\\leq 2n^n, \\) with equality precisely when \\(V\\) is a smooth quadric …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\) be an \\(n\\)-dimensional K-semistable \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Fano variety, where \\(n\\geq 2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Fano variety is normal, projective and klt, with ample \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier anticanonical divisor.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li\">If \\(V\\) is singular, then\n  \\[\n  (-K_V)^n\\leq\n  2\\left(\\frac{n^2-1}{n}\\right)^n&lt;2n^n.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(V\\not\\simeq\\mathbb P^n\\), then\n  \\[\n  (-K_V)^n\\leq 2n^n,\n  \\]\n  with equality precisely when \\(V\\) is a smooth quadric\n  \\(Q^n\\subset\\mathbb P^{n+1}\\) or \\(\\mathbb P^1\\times\\mathbb P^{n-1}\\).\n  These descriptions coincide for \\(n=2\\).</p><p>The smaller singular bound is not accompanied by an assertion of sharpness or a classification of its equality cases.</p>", "proof": "<p>For every closed point \\(x\\) of a K-semistable \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Fano \\(n\\)-fold, the local-to-global comparison [33, Theorem 21] gives\n\\[\n(-K_V)^n\n\\leq\\left(\\frac{n+1}{n}\\right)^n\n\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,V).\n\\]\nK-semistability and Ding semistability agree here [33, Theorem 13(2)].</p><p>If \\(V\\) is singular, apply Theorem 1 at a singular closed point:\n\\[\n(-K_V)^n\n\\leq\n\\left(\\frac{n+1}{n}\\right)^n2(n-1)^n\n=\n2\\left(\\frac{n^2-1}{n}\\right)^n\n&lt;2n^n.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(V\\) is smooth and not projective space, Li–Miao [28, Theorem 1.1] gives the bound \\(2n^n\\), with equality exactly for a smooth quadric or \\(\\mathbb P^1\\times\\mathbb P^{n-1}\\). Both admit Kähler–Einstein metrics, respectively the homogeneous quadric metric and a suitably scaled Fubini–Study product metric, so are K-polystable [3]. Their degrees are\n\\[\n(-K_{Q^n})^n=(nH)^n=2n^n,\n\\]\n\\[\n(-K_{\\mathbb P^1\\times\\mathbb P^{n-1}})^n\n=(2H_1+nH_2)^n=2n^n.\n\\]\nFor \\(n=2\\), a smooth quadric surface is \\(\\mathbb P^1\\times\\mathbb P^1\\). The strict singular bound excludes all further equality cases.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "The ODP minimum", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(0,Q_n)=2(n-1)^n. \\) The same value holds at every analytic ordinary double point of dimension \\(n\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq 2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  Q_n=\\operatorname{Spec}\n  \\mathbb C[z_0,\\ldots,z_n]/(z_0^2+\\cdots+z_n^2).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use existence and uniqueness up to positive scaling of a normalized-volume minimizer [51, 52], and invariance of normalized volume under isomorphic completed local rings [34, Lemma 2.15].</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(0,Q_n)=2(n-1)^n.\n\\]\nThe same value holds at every analytic ordinary double point of dimension \\(n\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The quadratic cone is klt: blowing up its vertex resolves it with smooth exceptional quadric of log discrepancy \\(n-1&gt;0\\).</p><p>Normalize a minimizing valuation \\(v\\) by its discrepancy. Uniqueness makes it invariant under all automorphisms, in particular cone scalings and \\(\\operatorname{SO}(n+1,\\mathbb C)\\). If\n\\[\nR=\\mathbb C[z_0,\\ldots,z_n]/(q),\n\\qquad q=\\sum_i z_i^2,\n\\]\nthen its ordinary degree-\\(j\\) piece \\(R_j\\) is the irreducible representation of harmonic polynomials of degree \\(j\\). Indeed, with \\(P_j\\) denoting homogeneous polynomials and \\(H_j\\) harmonic polynomials,\n\\[\nP_j=H_j\\oplus qP_{j-2}.\n\\]\nThe representation is irreducible already under the compact group \\(\\operatorname{SO}(n+1)\\); this includes \\(n=2,3\\). The exception with two ambient variables lies outside the range considered.</p><p>For each cutoff \\(t\\), the invariant subspace\n\\[\n\\{f\\in R_j:v(f)\\geq t\\}\n\\]\nis either zero or all of \\(R_j\\). Thus every nonzero element of \\(R_j\\) has a common value \\(a_j\\). For \\(0\\neq f\\in R_1\\), multiplicativity gives \\(a_j=ja_1\\). A finite Vandermonde combination of cone scalings isolates every homogeneous component of a cutoff, so\n\\[\nv\\!\\left(\\sum_j g_j\\right)\n=a_1\\min\\{j:g_j\\neq 0\\}.\n\\]\nThe same assertion holds after localization at the vertex, since denominators nonzero there have value zero.</p><p>The minimizer is therefore proportional to the ordinary degree valuation. Its discrepancy is \\(n-1\\), by hypersurface adjunction, and its volume is the multiplicity \\(2\\). Hence the minimum is \\(2(n-1)^n\\). Completion invariance gives the assertion for analytic ordinary double points.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Cone reduction, with the induction setting of Section 2.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exists a singular boundary-zero K-polystable Fano cone \\(C=\\operatorname{Spec}S\\), with vertex \\(o\\), such that \\( d(o,C)=d(x,X), \\qquad \\operatorname{edim}(X,x)\\leq\\operatorname{edim}(C,o). \\) The puncture …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  d(x,X)=\\frac{\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)}{n^n},\n  \\qquad\n  p_k=2\\left(\\frac{k-1}{k}\\right)^k.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n=N+1\\geq 5\\). Assume the ordinary-double-point theorem in dimensions smaller than \\(n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(x\\in X\\) be a singular boundary-zero klt \\(n\\)-dimensional germ with\n  \\[\n  d(x,X)\\geq p_n.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The lower-dimensional bases used in the paper are the surface quotient calculation, the threefold theorem [36, Theorem 1.3(1)], and the following companion input:\n  \n  **Original Theorem 2.4: The fourfold gap.** For every singular boundary-zero complex algebraic klt fourfold germ,\n  \\[\n  \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\\leq 162,\n  \\]\n  with equality exactly at the analytic ordinary double point. This is [44, Theorem 1.1], used as an input, not proved in this paper.</p><p class=\"li\">Established inputs used here and subsequently are:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. A minimizing valuation exists, is quasi-monomial, and has finitely generated associated graded. Its K-semistable Fano cone admits a special equivariant degeneration to a K-polystable Fano cone preserving the minimizing Reeb vector and normalized volume [52, Theorem 1.2; 31, Theorem 1.2].</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. A discrepancy-normalized minimizer is invariant under all germ automorphisms [51].</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. Normalized volume is lower semicontinuous in the stated \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Gorenstein flat families and when points move in a fixed klt variety [5]. One has \\(d\\leq 1\\), with equality exactly at smooth points [36].</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">4. For a finite Galois crepant morphism of klt germs with one point over the marked point,\n     \\[\n     \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(y,Y)\n     =\\deg(f)\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X).\n     \\tag{2.2}\n     \\]\n     This is used for quotients without reflections and cyclic canonical index covers [51].</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">5. Isomorphic completed local rings have the same normalized volume [34, Lemma 2.15]; in particular, étale germs at complex closed points have the same invariant.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exists a singular boundary-zero K-polystable Fano cone \\(C=\\operatorname{Spec}S\\), with vertex \\(o\\), such that\n\\[\nd(o,C)=d(x,X),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{edim}(X,x)\\leq\\operatorname{edim}(C,o).\n\\]\nThe puncture \\(C\\setminus\\{o\\}\\) is smooth, \\(S\\) is a normal Gorenstein ring, and its canonical module has a homogeneous generator.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply stable degeneration to a minimizer at \\(x\\), then the K-polystable special degeneration. In the first passage the Reeb valuation has the same volume and discrepancy as the original minimizer [29, Lemma 4.10]. In the second, equivariant flatness preserves character-space dimensions and hence Reeb volume; the relative canonical character, and therefore discrepancy, is constant. The characterization of K-semistability [29, Theorem 1.3] shows that the final Reeb valuation minimizes over all centered valuations. Thus both passages preserve the infimum.</p><p>Lemma 2.2 gives the embedding-dimension inequality for the first passage; upper semicontinuity along the vertex section gives it for the second. In particular, the final vertex is singular.</p><p>Choose any positive integral grading of \\(S\\). For \\(P\\neq o\\), its orbit specializes to \\(o\\), so lower semicontinuity gives\n\\[\nd(o,C)\\leq d(P,C).\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\nChoose homogeneous \\(f\\) of degree \\(e&gt;0\\) with \\(c=f(P)\\neq 0\\), and set\n\\[\nY_P=\\{f=c\\}\\subset C_f.\n\\]\nThe action map\n\\[\nY_P\\times\\mathbb G_m\\longrightarrow C_f,\n\\qquad (Q,a)\\longmapsto a\\cdot Q,\n\\]\nis the finite étale cover obtained by adjoining \\(a^e=f/c\\). Thus the germ at \\(P\\) is étale-locally the product of the \\(N\\)-dimensional klt slice \\(Y_P\\) and a smooth curve. If \\(P\\) were singular, the slice would be singular, and induction, Lemma 2.1 and étale invariance would give\n\\[\nd(P,C)\\leq p_N&lt;p_n\\leq d(o,C),\n\\]\ncontrary to (2.5).</p><p>Here \\(p_k\\) is strictly increasing: for real \\(t&gt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl[t\\log(1-1/t)\\bigr]\n=\\log(1-1/t)+\\frac1{t-1}&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(q\\) be the canonical index at \\(o\\). The cyclic index cover is quasi-étale, crepant and klt of degree \\(q\\), with one point over \\(o\\). Indeed, in its algebra of reflexive canonical powers modulo \\(q\\), a nonnilpotent homogeneous element of nonzero residue degree in the vertex fiber would have a power returning to degree zero that is a unit. Its section would trivialize a proper positive canonical power, contrary to minimality of \\(q\\). Thus all nonzero residue-degree pieces are nilpotent in the fiber.</p><p>The finite degree formula and \\(d\\leq1\\) imply \\(d(o,C)\\leq1/q\\). Since \\(p_n&gt;1/2\\), one has \\(q=1\\).</p><p>Klt singularities are Cohen–Macaulay. The canonical module is invertible at the vertex. A homogeneous element generating its one-dimensional fiber there generates after localization. Its graded cokernel has empty support, because every nonempty grading-invariant closed support contains the vertex. Torsion-freeness gives injectivity. Thus \\(\\omega_S\\) is free with a homogeneous generator.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "A smooth factor", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( d((y,0),Y\\times\\mathbb A^1)\\leq d(y,Y). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(y\\in Y\\) be an \\(a\\)-dimensional klt germ, where \\(a\\geq 1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize density in dimension \\(a\\) by division by \\(a^a\\), and in dimension \\(a+1\\) by division by \\((a+1)^{a+1}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nd((y,0),Y\\times\\mathbb A^1)\\leq d(y,Y).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Take a divisorial test valuation \\(v\\) centered at \\(y\\), with discrepancy \\(A&gt;0\\) and volume \\(\\sigma\\), and set \\(b=A/a\\). Its Gauss extension is\n\\[\n(v\\oplus b)\\!\\left(\\sum_i f_it^i\\right)\n=\\min_i\\{v(f_i)+ib\\}.\n\\]\nOn a log resolution this is monomial along the divisor defining \\(v\\) and \\(t=0\\), with discrepancy \\(A+b\\).</p><p>Completion does not change finite colengths, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}\n\\bigl(R[[t]]/\\mathfrak a_k(v\\oplus b)\\bigr)\n=\n\\sum_{0\\leq i&lt;k/b}\n\\operatorname{length}(R/\\mathfrak a_{k-ib}(v)).\n\\]\nThe asymptotic defining \\(\\sigma\\), followed by a Riemann sum, gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}(v\\oplus b)=\\frac{\\sigma}{b}.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\frac{(A+A/a)^{a+1}}{(a+1)^{a+1}}\n\\frac{a\\sigma}{A}\n=\n\\frac{A^a\\sigma}{a^a}.\n\\]\nTake the infimum over divisorial tests on \\(Y\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lifting generators", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\operatorname{edim}(R)\\leq \\operatorname{edim}(\\operatorname{gr}_vR). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(R\\) be the local ring of a klt germ.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v\\) have finite discrepancy and finitely generated positively graded associated graded ring \\(\\operatorname{gr}_vR\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{edim}(R)\\leq\n\\operatorname{edim}(\\operatorname{gr}_vR).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose minimal homogeneous algebra generators\n\\(\\bar f_1,\\ldots,\\bar f_e\\) of positive value and lift them to\n\\(f_1,\\ldots,f_e\\in\\mathfrak m\\).</p><p>For \\(g\\in\\mathfrak m\\), its initial form is a homogeneous polynomial in the \\(\\bar f_i\\). Subtracting the corresponding polynomial in the \\(f_i\\) annihilates the remainder or strictly increases its value. Every occurring positive value belongs to the semigroup generated by \\(v(f_i)&gt;0\\), so only finitely many such values lie below any fixed bound.</p><p>The Izumi inequality [26, Theorem 3.1] gives\n\\[\nv(g)\\leq c\\,\\operatorname{ord}_{\\mathfrak m}(g)\n\\]\nfor a fixed \\(c\\). A nonzero remainder of value greater than \\(c\\) therefore belongs to \\(\\mathfrak m^2\\). The cancellation process terminates modulo \\(\\mathfrak m^2\\), and every polynomial subtracted is, modulo \\(\\mathfrak m^2\\), a linear combination of the \\(f_i\\). Their classes span \\(\\mathfrak m/\\mathfrak m^2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are primitive positive integral cocharacters \\(\\zeta_i\\), with canonical weights \\(r_i=A(\\zeta_i)>0\\), satisfying \\( \\zeta_i=\\frac{r_i}{n}\\xi+\\epsilon_i, \\qquad \\epsilon_i\\longrightarrow0. \\) Their degree …", "statement": "<p>**Integral gradings and orbifold quotients: original Lemmas 2.6 and 2.7.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a cone \\(C=\\operatorname{Spec}S\\) supplied by Proposition 2, of dimension \\(n=N+1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\) be an effective algebraic torus of automorphisms containing the minimizing Reeb vector \\(\\xi\\), normalized by \\(A(\\xi)=n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a \\(T\\)-eigen-generator \\(\\Omega_C\\) of \\(\\omega_S\\). Its character defines the linear discrepancy functional \\(A\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Function weights use \\(g(aP)=a^jg(P)\\); \\(dg\\) has the same weight.</p><p class=\"li\">A Reeb vector is positive on every nonconstant homogeneous function.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are primitive positive integral cocharacters \\(\\zeta_i\\), with canonical weights \\(r_i=A(\\zeta_i)&gt;0\\), satisfying\n\\[\n\\zeta_i=\\frac{r_i}{n}\\xi+\\epsilon_i,\n\\qquad \\epsilon_i\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nTheir degree valuations have normalized volumes tending to\n\\(\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)\\). If the Reeb ray is rational, the sequence can be constant and exact; otherwise \\(r_i\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>For each primitive positive integral grading \\(\\zeta\\), of canonical weight \\(r\\), the quotient\n\\[\nB=[(C\\setminus\\{o\\})/\\mathbb G_m]\n\\]\nis a smooth effective proper orbifold of dimension \\(N\\), with projective coarse space \\(\\operatorname{Proj}S\\). It has a faithful ample weight-one line bundle \\(L\\) satisfying\n\\[\nS_j=H^0(B,jL),\n\\qquad -K_B=rL.\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\nAll stabilizers are finite cyclic groups, acting faithfully on a smooth transverse uniformizer and faithfully on the fiber of \\(L\\).</p><p>For fixed homogeneous algebra generators, their \\(\\zeta_i\\)-degrees lie between \\(cr_i\\) and \\(Cr_i\\), for fixed positive constants. The largest punctured-cone stabilizer order is \\(O(r_i)\\).</p><p>A “sufficiently fast grading” means a sufficiently far term of such a sequence, including the exact constant sequence on a rational ray. Constants may depend on the fixed cone.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 3.1 supplies the approximating integral gradings and their quantitative properties. Lemma 3.2 supplies the orbifold quotient for each grading. The stack and its faithful weight-one line bundle are retained; no replacement by a coarse line bundle is made before stabilizers are shown to be trivial.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Fast primitive approximations", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The cocharacters and convergence assertions in Proposition 3 exist, including the generator-degree bounds and the estimate \\(m_{\\max,i}=O(r_i)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the cone, torus, discrepancy functional and normalized Reeb vector of Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The cocharacters and convergence assertions in Proposition 3 exist, including the generator-degree bounds and the estimate \\(m_{\\max,i}=O(r_i)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Recurrence in the quotient of real cocharacter space by its integral lattice gives \\(t_i\\to\\infty\\) and integral cocharacters\n\\[\nq_i=t_i\\xi+e_i,\\qquad e_i\\to0.\n\\]\nThese may be obtained by simultaneous Dirichlet approximation or returns to zero of the corresponding one-parameter subgroup in its compact closure.</p><p>Let \\(d_i\\) be the gcd of the coordinates of \\(q_i\\) in a lattice basis, and put \\(\\zeta_i=q_i/d_i\\). Then\n\\[\n\\zeta_i-\\frac{A(\\zeta_i)}n\\xi\n=\n\\frac1{d_i}\\left(e_i-\\frac{A(e_i)}n\\xi\\right)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThe cocharacters are positive for large \\(i\\). If \\(r_i=A(\\zeta_i)\\) had a bounded subsequence, the displayed convergence would make \\(\\zeta_i\\) bounded there; a constant lattice subsequence would force \\(\\xi\\) to have rational ray. On a rational ray use its primitive generator.</p><p>For fixed homogeneous generators of characters \\(\\chi_j\\), positivity of \\(\\chi_j(\\xi)\\) gives two-sided degree bounds proportional to \\(r_i\\). The normalized cocharacters \\((n/r_i)\\zeta_i\\) converge to \\(\\xi\\). Comparison on the finite generator-character set extends to every occurring character, since each is a nonnegative integral sum of generator characters. The corresponding valuation ideals are squeezed between those of \\(\\operatorname{wt}_\\xi\\) with cutoff factors tending to one. Volumes converge, and the normalized discrepancies are all \\(n\\).</p><p>At a nonvertex point some generator is nonzero. Its cyclic stabilizer order divides every nonvanishing generator degree, so is at most the largest generator degree.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "The quotient orbifold", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The quotient \\(B\\), line bundle \\(L\\), section identities, canonical identity and stabilizer properties stated in Proposition 3 hold.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the cone of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a primitive positive integral grading \\(\\zeta\\), and put \\(r=A(\\zeta)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The quotient \\(B\\), line bundle \\(L\\), section identities, canonical identity and stabilizer properties stated in Proposition 3 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>The smooth puncture has finite stabilizers under the positive grading, so the quotient is smooth and Deligne–Mumford. Its coarse space is \\(\\operatorname{Proj}S\\); a sufficiently divisible positive power of the weight-one line descends to an ample coarse line bundle. This also gives properness.</p><p>The grading is effective because the cocharacter is primitive in an effective torus. A stabilizer acts trivially on the orbit tangent line. If an element also acted trivially on the transverse tangent space, finite-order formal linearization in characteristic zero would make it the identity near that point, and hence on the irreducible cone. This contradicts effectiveness. The transverse representation is therefore faithful, and so is the weight-one character of the subgroup of \\(\\mathbb G_m\\).</p><p>Sections of \\(jL\\) are homogeneous degree-\\(j\\) functions on the puncture. Normality extends them across the vertex. Contracting \\(\\Omega_C\\) with the Euler field gives a horizontal top form of character \\(r\\), hence \\(K_B=-rL\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Small-stabilizer classification", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For all sufficiently large \\(i\\), every stabilizer is trivial and \\( (B_i,L_i)\\simeq \\begin{cases} (\\mathbb P^N,\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb P^N}(1)),&r_i=N+1,\\\\ (Q^N,\\mathcal O_{Q^N}(1)),&r_i=N. \\end{cases} \\) Consequently …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C=\\operatorname{Spec}S\\) be the fixed normal Gorenstein K-polystable cone of dimension \\(n=N+1\\), where \\(N\\geq4\\), with smooth puncture.</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize its minimizing Reeb vector by \\(A(\\xi)=n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(T\\)-eigen-generators \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_q\\), with characters \\(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let primitive positive integral gradings satisfy\n  \\[\n  \\zeta_i=\\frac{r_i}{n}\\xi+\\epsilon_i,\n  \\qquad \\|\\epsilon_i\\|\\to0,\n  \\qquad\n  m_{\\max}(\\zeta_i)\\leq\\frac{r_i}{N}.\n  \\tag{3.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For each grading use the quotient orbifold \\(B_i\\) and line \\(L_i\\) of Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large \\(i\\), every stabilizer is trivial and\n\\[\n(B_i,L_i)\\simeq\n\\begin{cases}\n(\\mathbb P^N,\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb P^N}(1)),&amp;r_i=N+1,\\\\\n(Q^N,\\mathcal O_{Q^N}(1)),&amp;r_i=N.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nConsequently \\(C\\) is respectively smooth or the standard ordinary double point cone. This includes the constant exact primitive grading on a rational Reeb ray.</p>", "proof": "<p>For each grading choose the curve of Lemma 4.1. In its exceptional case, \\(r/m=N\\) and the tangent action is trivial. Faithfulness from Proposition 3 implies \\(m=1\\), hence \\(r=N\\), and \\(m_{\\max}\\leq r/N\\) forces every stabilizer to be trivial.</p><p>Otherwise Lemmas 4.2 and 4.3 apply. Lemma 4.5 and \\(r/m\\leq n\\) give\n\\[\n0\\leq nm-r\\leq nc\\|\\epsilon\\|.\n\\]\nThe left side is integral, so \\(nm=r\\) for sufficiently far gradings, immediately on an exact rational grading.</p><p>Now\n\\[\nn=\\dim H\n\\geq n+\\frac rm-\\sum_j\\frac{b'_j}{m}\n=2n-\\sum_j\\frac{b'_j}{m}\n\\geq n.\n\\]\nAll equalities hold, so every \\(b'_j=m\\). Faithfulness again gives \\(m=1\\), and \\(r=n=N+1\\). Since\n\\[\nm_{\\max}\\leq\\frac{N+1}{N}&lt;2,\n\\]\nall stabilizers are trivial.</p><p>Thus \\(B\\) is a smooth projective Fano \\(N\\)-fold, \\(L\\) an actual ample line bundle, and \\(-K_B=rL\\) with \\(r=N\\) or \\(N+1\\). The ample-line-bundle form of Kobayashi–Ochiai [22], also supplied by [2], gives precisely the stated polarized quadric or projective space. The identity\n\\[\nS=\\bigoplus_{j\\geq0}H^0(B,jL)\n\\]\nthen identifies the cone as the standard quadratic cone or affine space.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The minimum exists and satisfies \\( e=1,\\qquad \\ell=m,\\qquad N\\leq r/m\\leq n. \\) Every component through \\(F\\) of the scheme of homogeneous maps \\(F'\\) satisfying \\(F'(0,1)=P\\) has dimension at least …", "statement": "<p>**Minimal-curve reduction of Section 3.1 and original Lemma 3.2: Deformations with a fixed lift.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix one grading from Proposition 4 and write \\(r=A(\\zeta)\\), \\(m_{\\max}\\leq r/N\\), and \\(-K_B=rL\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Among representable nonconstant maps from weighted lines with at most one stack point, choose one of least positive \\(L\\)-degree:\n  \\[\n  f:\\mathbb P(1,\\ell)\\to B,\\qquad f^*L\\simeq\\mathcal O(e).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m\\) be the stabilizer order of its value at infinity.</p><p class=\"li\">After identifying the pulled-back torsor, write\n  \\[\n  F:\\mathbb A^2(1,m)\\to C,\n  \\qquad\n  F(tx,t^my)=t\\cdot F(x,y),\n  \\qquad P=F(0,1).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b'_1,\\ldots,b'_n\\in\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}\\) represent the characters on \\(T_{C,P}\\), with the trivial character represented by \\(m\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The minimum exists and satisfies\n\\[\ne=1,\\qquad \\ell=m,\\qquad N\\leq r/m\\leq n.\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nEvery component through \\(F\\) of the scheme of homogeneous maps \\(F'\\) satisfying \\(F'(0,1)=P\\) has dimension at least\n\\[\n\\chi=n+\\frac rm-\\sum_{j=1}^n\\frac{b'_j}{m}.\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nThis dimension is at least \\(n\\), except possibly when \\(r/m=N\\) and the entire tangent action at \\(P\\) is trivial.</p>", "proof": "<p>The orbifold rational-curve theorem [30, Proposition 2.6] supplies a representable nonconstant map from \\(\\mathbb P(1,\\ell)\\), with only a possible stack point at infinity, whose anticanonical degree plus inverse age there is at most \\(N+1=n\\). In particular, its anticanonical degree is at most \\(n\\).</p><p>Representability embeds source inertia into target inertia, so denominators of these \\(L\\)-degrees divide integers at most \\(m_{\\max}\\). Hence a least positive degree exists. Since \\(\\ell\\mid m\\), minimality and the existence theorem give \\(re/\\ell\\leq n\\), while \\(r/m\\geq N\\). Therefore\n\\[\n1\\leq \\frac{em}{\\ell}\n=\\frac{re/\\ell}{r/m}\n\\leq\\frac{N+1}{N}&lt;2.\n\\]\nThe middle expression is an integer, giving (3.4).</p><p>Passing to the grading torsor gives \\(F\\) on \\(\\mathbb A^2\\setminus\\{0\\}\\). Its coordinate functions extend across the origin by normality and affineness. Conversely, the degree-one torsor identification makes such maps avoiding the vertex outside the origin representable.</p><p>Homogeneous maps form a finite-type affine scheme: generator images are weighted homogeneous polynomials of fixed degrees, and the ring relations give polynomial equations in their coefficients. Avoiding the vertex outside the origin is open, as checked on the proper weighted line.</p><p>Let \\(E\\) be the rank-\\(n\\) bundle on \\(\\mathbb P(1,m)\\) descending \\(F^*T_{C^\\circ}\\). Its determinant is \\(\\mathcal O(r)\\), so \\(\\deg E=r/m\\). With source and torsor fixed, deformations fixing the residual gerbe \\(D_\\infty\\) have tangent and obstruction spaces\n\\[\nH^0(E(-D_\\infty)),\n\\qquad\nH^1(E(-D_\\infty)),\n\\]\nby the section obstruction theory [49]. Their Euler characteristic bounds every component dimension from below.</p><p>Orbifold Riemann–Roch gives\n\\[\n\\chi(E)=n+\\deg E-\\operatorname{age}(E_\\infty).\n\\]\nRestriction to \\(D_\\infty\\) subtracts the invariant-fiber dimension, which is exactly the effect of representing each trivial character by \\(m\\) rather than zero. This yields (3.5).</p><p>Since \\(\\sum_j b'_j/m\\leq n\\), one has \\(\\chi\\geq r/m\\). The Euler characteristic is integral. If \\(r/m&gt;N\\), it is at least \\(N+1=n\\); if \\(r/m=N\\) and some character is nontrivial, the inequality is strict and the same conclusion holds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Finite evaluation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Evaluation is finite and surjective, and its fiber over \\(o\\) is supported at the unique map obtained by deleting all terms involving \\(x\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Lemma 4.1 and assume its exceptional case does not occur.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be the reduced closure of an irreducible component of the open locus of maps avoiding the vertex outside the origin, with fixed value \\(P\\) at \\((0,1)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{ev}:H\\to C,\\qquad F'\\mapsto F'(1,0).\n  \\tag{3.7}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Evaluation is finite and surjective, and its fiber over \\(o\\) is supported at the unique map obtained by deleting all terms involving \\(x\\). Moreover \\(\\dim H=n\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 gives \\(\\dim H\\geq n\\). Precomposition by \\((x,y)\\mapsto(ax,y)\\) preserves the component because \\(\\mathbb G_m\\) is connected. It gives a positive grading on \\(H\\): coefficients involving \\(x\\) have positive weight, while pure-\\(y\\) coefficients are fixed by \\(P\\). All maps contract to the same map without \\(x\\)-terms.</p><p>Take \\(F_0\\in H\\) with \\(F_0(1,0)=o\\). Choose a pointed normalized curve through it, generically in the vertex-avoiding locus. The generic map gives a twisted stable graph map to \\(B\\times\\mathbb P^1\\), with second map the coarse identification of the weighted line and with infinity marked. After finite base change, properness of twisted stable maps [1, Theorem 4.3] gives a limit.</p><p>The unique component of second degree one is horizontal and maps isomorphically to \\(\\mathbb P^1\\). All other components form rational trees. The first map on the horizontal component is the rational map defined by \\(F_0\\), after canceling basepoints.</p><p>A positive-degree tail occurs over the finite basepoint \\([1:0]\\). Indeed, take sufficiently divisible \\(k\\), with \\(m\\mid k\\), such that \\(kL\\) descends to a very ample coarse line bundle. The limiting projective coordinates are binary forms of degree \\(k/m\\), with a common zero at \\([1:0]\\), but not all zero since \\(F_0(0,1)=P\\neq o\\). Canceling their common divisor loses positive degree there. A general hyperplane avoiding the canceled value has the lost positive number of inverse-image points specializing there, so a tree over that point has positive first degree.</p><p>Choose a terminal leaf of that tree. It has one node and no markings. If its first degree were zero, it would be constant in both factors and unstable. Thus it has positive first degree, and its normalization has at most one stack point. Projection to \\(B\\) remains representable because \\(\\mathbb P^1\\) has trivial inertia. Its \\(L\\)-degree is at least the original minimum \\(1/m\\). Since total degree is \\(1/m\\) and every component has nonnegative degree, the leaf consumes the full first degree. The horizontal component is constant at the coarse image of \\(P\\).</p><p>It follows that the image of \\(F_0\\) lies in the orbit closure of \\(P\\). Its normalization is \\(\\mathbb A^1\\), with parameter of grading weight \\(m\\), normalized to equal one at \\(P\\): \\(m\\) is the gcd of degrees of coordinates nonzero at \\(P\\). The normal source \\(\\mathbb A^2\\) factors through this normalization, since the parameter is integral over the orbit-curve ring. Its pullback is a weight-\\(m\\) polynomial\n\\[\ny+cx^m.\n\\]\nEvaluation at \\((1,0)\\) is the vertex only if \\(c=0\\). Thus the vertex fiber is supported at the unique contracted map.</p><p>If \\(R_H\\) is the positive coordinate ring of \\(H\\), then \\(R_H/S_+R_H\\) is finite-dimensional. Lift a homogeneous basis; induction on positive degree shows that the lifts generate \\(R_H\\) as an \\(S\\)-module. Hence evaluation is finite. Its closed image has dimension \\(\\dim H\\geq n\\), so is all of the integral \\(n\\)-dimensional cone \\(C\\), and \\(\\dim H=n\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "A tensor of negative integral weight", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a nonzero algebraic section \\( s\\in H^0(C^\\circ,\\operatorname{Sym}^bT_{C^\\circ}) \\) of integral-grading weight \\(-bm\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Lemma 4.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b\\) be the degree of its finite evaluation map.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a nonzero algebraic section\n\\[\ns\\in H^0(C^\\circ,\\operatorname{Sym}^bT_{C^\\circ})\n\\]\nof integral-grading weight \\(-bm\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The source automorphisms\n\\[\n(x,y)\\mapsto(x,y+tx^m)\n\\]\npreserve the map scheme, fixed value and open vertex-avoiding locus, hence preserve \\(H\\). Their vector field is nonzero: a coordinate nonzero at \\(P\\) contains a nonzero pure power of \\(y\\), whose derivative is nonzero in characteristic zero.</p><p>Differentiating evaluation gives a regular vector along evaluation over \\(C^\\circ\\). It is generically nonzero because the finite dominant evaluation is generically separable and has injective differential on a nonempty smooth open set. In a target coordinate of degree \\(d\\), its component is \\(\\partial_yF'(1,0)\\), of coefficient degree \\(d-m\\). Thus its derivation weight is \\(-m\\).</p><p>Over the generic point, take the symmetric product of these vectors on all \\(b\\) sheets. The product is nonzero and invariant under sheet permutations, so descends to \\(\\mathbb C(C)\\). It is regular: on a smooth affine tangent-trivializing open set, its coefficients are integral over the base ring and lie in its fraction field. Normality makes them regular. This does not require \\(H\\) to be normal. The weight is \\(-bm\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "Tensor weight bound", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\tau\\geq-b. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the fixed K-polystable cone and normalized minimizing Reeb vector of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b\\geq1\\), and let \\(s\\neq0\\) be a homogeneous algebraic section of \\(\\operatorname{Sym}^bT_{C^\\circ}\\), of \\(\\xi\\)-weight \\(\\tau\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Vector fields are weighted as derivations.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\tau\\geq-b.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The Fano-cone Yau–Tian–Donaldson theorem [8] supplies a Ricci-flat Kähler cone metric on the smooth puncture. With \\(A(\\xi)=n\\), real homothetic dilation multiplies the metric by the square of its factor.</p><p>The induced Chern connection on \\(\\operatorname{Sym}^bT_{C^\\circ}\\) has zero mean curvature. For \\(u=|s|^2\\), the holomorphic Bochner formula and Cauchy–Schwarz give\n\\[\n\\Delta u=|\\nabla^{1,0}s|^2\\geq0,\n\\qquad\n|\\partial u|^2\\leq u\\Delta u.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\log(u+a)\\) and \\((u+a)^{p/2}\\) are subharmonic for \\(a,p&gt;0\\). Letting \\(a\\downarrow0\\) shows that \\(|s|^p\\) is distributionally subharmonic, including across its zero set.</p><p>The norm of a rank-\\(b\\) contravariant tensor of derivation weight \\(\\tau\\) has radial degree \\(\\tau+b\\). If \\(\\tau+b&lt;0\\), choose \\(p&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\nd=p(\\tau+b)\\in(2-2n,0).\n\\]\nWriting \\(|s|^p=\\rho^d\\varphi\\) on the compact smooth link \\(M=\\{\\rho=1\\}\\), one has \\(\\varphi\\geq0\\) and \\(\\int_M\\varphi&gt;0\\). The cone-Laplacian formula gives\n\\[\n\\Delta(\\rho^d\\varphi)\n=\\rho^{d-2}\\bigl(d(d+2n-2)\\varphi+\\Delta_M\\varphi\\bigr).\n\\]\nIntegrating the angular term, then testing against a nonnegative radial function supported in an annulus, contradicts subharmonicity because \\(d(d+2n-2)&lt;0\\). Hence \\(\\tau+b\\geq0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.5", "orig": "Character estimate", "depth": 1, "snippet": "It has a nonzero torus eigencomponent of character \\(\\chi\\) satisfying \\( \\|\\chi\\|\\leq cb \\) for a constant independent of \\(b,m\\) and the nearby grading.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\zeta=(r/n)\\xi+\\epsilon\\) for a sufficiently close grading.</p><p class=\"li\">Let a nonzero rank-\\(b\\) tensor on \\(C^\\circ\\) have \\(\\zeta\\)-weight \\(-bm\\), where \\(m&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>It has a nonzero torus eigencomponent of character \\(\\chi\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\|\\chi\\|\\leq cb\n\\]\nfor a constant independent of \\(b,m\\) and the nearby grading. For the tensor of Lemma 4.3,\n\\[\nm\\leq\\frac rn+c\\|\\epsilon\\|.\n\\tag{3.8}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The fixed generator degrees satisfy\n\\[\nc_1r\\leq d_j=\\alpha_j(\\zeta)\\leq c_2r.\n\\tag{3.9}\n\\]\nPairing a tensor with all ordered \\(b\\)-tuples of the spanning differentials \\(df_j\\) embeds its section space into a finite tuple of regular functions on \\(C^\\circ\\), which extend over \\(o\\). This also proves local finiteness of the torus action and gives a nonzero eigencomponent retaining the specified \\(\\zeta\\)-weight.</p><p>For a nonzero pairing, its function character is\n\\[\n\\beta=\\chi+\\alpha_{j_1}+\\cdots+\\alpha_{j_b},\n\\]\nand its degree satisfies\n\\[\n0\\leq d=-bm+d_{j_1}+\\cdots+d_{j_b}\\leq c_2rb.\n\\]\nAn equivariant polynomial lift in the generators consists of monomials of character \\(\\beta\\), each containing at most\n\\[\nd/(c_1r)\\leq(c_2/c_1)b\n\\]\nfactors. Thus \\(\\|\\beta\\|=O(b)\\), and \\(\\|\\chi\\|=O(b)\\).</p><p>Put \\(\\tau=\\chi(\\xi)\\). Lemma 4.4 gives \\(\\tau\\geq-b\\). Therefore\n\\[\n-bm=\\chi(\\zeta)\n=\\frac rn\\tau+\\chi(\\epsilon)\n\\geq-\\frac rn b-cb\\|\\epsilon\\|.\n\\]\nDivide by \\(b\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Jet setup", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(\\Gamma\\) is additive with one-dimensional leaves and generated group exactly \\(\\Lambda\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the cone of Proposition 2, of dimension \\(n=N+1\\), with \\(A(\\xi)=n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Consider sufficiently fast gradings \\(\\zeta=(r/n)\\xi+\\epsilon\\), and choose a point of maximal stabilizer order\n  \\[\n  m\\geq r/N.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">On its quotient orbifold \\(B\\), choose algebraic equivariant étale parameters \\(z_1,\\ldots,z_N\\) of stabilizer characters \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_N\\in\\mathbb Z/m\\), and a frame of \\(L\\) of character \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A section of \\(jL\\) is represented by a Taylor series \\(F\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  F(\\varepsilon^{b_1}z_1,\\ldots,\\varepsilon^{b_N}z_N)\n  =\\varepsilon^jF(z).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Order monomials by total degree, then lexicographically, smallest first. Define\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma=\\{(j,\\gamma):0\\neq s\\in S_j,\\ \\operatorname{in}(s)=z^\\gamma\\},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\Lambda=\\left\\{(j,\\gamma)\\in\\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z^N:\n  j\\equiv\\sum_i b_i\\gamma_i\\pmod m\\right\\},\n  \\qquad\n  K=\\overline{\\operatorname{cone}(\\Gamma)}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  s=\\frac r{Nm}\\leq1,\\qquad\n  D_0=\\frac{Nj}{r}=\\frac{j}{sm},\n  \\]\n  and denote the images under \\((j,x)\\mapsto(Nj/r,x)\\) by\n  \\(\\widetilde K,\\widetilde\\Gamma,\\widetilde\\Lambda\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(c_0=(r,\\mathbf1)\\), where \\(\\mathbf1=(1,\\ldots,1)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(\\Gamma\\) is additive with one-dimensional leaves and generated group exactly \\(\\Lambda\\). Every finite compatible Taylor jet is realized in all sufficiently large degrees in each admissible progression.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\(K\\) is full-dimensional, closed and convex, contains the positive constant ray, projects onto the exponent orthant, and\n   \\[\n   K=\\overline{\\operatorname{int}K}.\n   \\]\n   Its bounded-degree sections are bounded, uniformly as\n   \\[\n   |\\gamma|:=\\sum_i\\gamma_i\\leq C_C\\,j/r\n   \\qquad ((j,\\gamma)\\in K).\n   \\tag{4.6}\n   \\]\n   Moreover\n   \\[\n   \\widetilde K\n   =\\{(D_0,x):x\\in\\mathbb R_{\\geq0}^N,\\ D_0\\geq f(x)\\},\n   \\tag{4.7}\n   \\]\n   where \\(f\\) is finite, convex, positively homogeneous, and\n   \\[\n   f(x)\\geq c_C|x|.\n   \\tag{4.8}\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The scaled lattice density is \\(s\\). For every linear functional \\(\\ell\\) strictly positive on \\(\\widetilde K\\setminus\\{0\\}\\),\n   \\[\n   \\#\\{\\gamma'\\in\\widetilde\\Gamma:\\ell(\\gamma')&lt;q\\}\n   =\n   s\\,\\operatorname{Leb}\\{\\widetilde K\\cap\\{\\ell&lt;1\\}\\}\\,q^n+o(q^n).\n   \\tag{4.9}\n   \\]\n   Saturation is not assumed.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. \\(c_0\\in\\Lambda\\), and its scaled coordinates are \\((N,\\mathbf1)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Leading monomials multiply and have scalar leading coefficient, giving additivity and one-dimensional leaves. Thus each index in a fixed degree accounts for one dimension of \\(S_j\\).</p><p>Choose \\(M\\) divisible by all stabilizer orders such that \\(ML\\) descends to an ample coarse line bundle. In each residue class \\(j_0\\bmod M\\), the kernel of a prescribed finite compatible jet map is coherent. Serre vanishing, or exact tame pushdown followed by Serre vanishing, makes the jet map surjective for every sufficiently large \\(j_0+kM\\). In particular, constant jets occur in all sufficiently large degrees divisible by \\(m\\), and every compatible monomial occurs as a leading term in sufficiently large degrees.</p><p>Differences of constant indices give \\((m,0)\\) in the generated group. Realized linear jets give \\((j_i,e_i)\\) with \\(j_i\\equiv b_i\\pmod m\\). These generate the full congruence lattice \\(\\Lambda\\). They also give full dimension, the positive constant ray and projection onto the exponent orthant.</p><p>For the uniform bound, fix torus eigen-generators and the corresponding affine embedding of \\(C\\). Their grading degrees are bounded below by a fixed positive multiple of \\(r\\). A degree-\\(j\\) section has a polynomial lift of ordinary degree at most \\(Cj/r\\). At a smooth punctured-cone point, its local vanishing order equals transverse vanishing order, because orbit coordinates contribute a unit.</p><p>Take a general affine-hyperplane complete-intersection curve through the point, smooth there and not contained in the function’s zero locus. Local intersection multiplicity is at least vanishing order, while Bézout bounds it by polynomial degree times the degree of the fixed embedded cone. This proves (4.6), first on \\(\\Gamma\\), then on \\(K\\).</p><p>The positive constant ray makes each nonempty exponent fiber an upper degree ray. Every exponent fiber is nonempty by nonnegative combinations of the linear-jet indices. Closedness and the degree bound attain its minimum. This gives the epigraph description and (4.8). A full-dimensional closed convex cone is the closure of its interior.</p><p>The original lattice has covolume \\(m\\). Scaling the degree coordinate gives covolume \\(Nm/r=1/s\\).</p><p>For nonsaturated counting, choose a finitely generated subsemigroup \\(\\Gamma_0\\) with group \\(\\Lambda\\), and cone \\(K_0\\). There is \\(c\\in\\Gamma_0\\) such that\n\\[\nc+(K_0\\cap\\Lambda)\\subset\\Gamma_0.\n\\tag{4.11}\n\\]\nIndeed, reduce coefficients in cones generated by linearly independent subsets of the finite generators. The remainders lie in finitely many bounded parallelepipeds, hence form a finite set of lattice points. Write every remainder \\(v=v_+-v_-\\) with \\(v_\\pm\\in\\Gamma_0\\), and take \\(c\\) to be the sum of the \\(v_-\\).</p><p>Lattice counting between the translated lattice cone and the lattice cone gives the leading volume for \\(\\Gamma_0\\). Exhaust \\(\\operatorname{int}K\\) by finite cones of indices of \\(\\Gamma\\), retaining a fixed finite set generating \\(\\Lambda\\). Every interior point belongs to one such cone: surround it by a small simplex and approximate its vertices by elements of \\(\\operatorname{cone}(\\Gamma)\\). These finite cones give the lower leading count; all lattice points of \\(K\\) give the upper count. Compact convex cutoff bodies have boundary of measure zero and are exhausted from the interior. This proves (4.9).</p><p>Finally, the canonical character on the uniformizer is minus the sum of coordinate characters in the line-bundle convention used. Since \\(-K_B=rL\\),\n\\[\nr\\equiv\\sum_i b_i\\pmod m,\n\\]\nso \\(c_0\\in\\Lambda\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The degree–Taylor-order tests below are centered divisorial valuations up to scaling, with the stated discrepancy and volume formulas.", "statement": "<p>**Discrepancy and moment tests: original Lemmas 4.2 and 4.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition 5 along the fixed-cone sequence of large-stabilizer gradings.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n  \\[\n  \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)\\geq2N^n.\n  \\tag{4.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X_1,\\ldots,X_N\\) be independent mean-one exponential random variables.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  B_0(x)=f(x)-|x|,\n  \\qquad\n  H=\\mathbb E e^{-B_0(X)}\n  =\\int_{\\mathbb R_{\\geq0}^N}e^{-f(x)}\\,dx.\n  \\tag{4.15}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The degree–Taylor-order tests below are centered divisorial valuations up to scaling, with the stated discrepancy and volume formulas. Along the sequence there are \\(\\eta_\\nu\\geq0\\), with \\(\\eta_\\nu\\to0\\), such that\n\\[\nH\\geq\\frac2s,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E[(1+B_0(X))e^{-B_0(X)}]\\geq-\\eta_\\nu H.\n\\tag{4.16}\n\\]\nFor an exact minimizing rational grading, one may take \\(\\eta_\\nu=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 6.1 establishes the tests and their exact numerical formulas. Lemma 6.2 applies the volume threshold and near-minimality of the grading sequence to obtain the two required moments.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "The degree and Taylor-order test", "depth": 1, "snippet": "This defines a valuation centered at \\(o\\), divisorial up to positive scaling, and \\( A_C(v_\\delta)=N+N\\delta, \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\delta\\in\\mathbb Q_{\\geq0}\\), define on homogeneous sums\n  \\[\n  v_\\delta\\!\\left(\\sum_j s_j\\right)\n  =\\min_{s_j\\neq0}\\left\\{\\frac{Nj}{r}\n  +\\delta\\,\\operatorname{ord}_z(s_j)\\right\\}.\n  \\tag{4.12}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>This defines a valuation centered at \\(o\\), divisorial up to positive scaling, and\n\\[\nA_C(v_\\delta)=N+N\\delta,\n\\tag{4.13}\n\\]\n\\[\nA_C(v_\\delta)^n\\operatorname{vol}(v_\\delta)\n=\nN^n(1+\\delta)^n s\n\\int_{\\mathbb R_{\\geq0}^N}\ne^{-f(x)-\\delta|x|}\\,dx.\n\\tag{4.14}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>On the local line-bundle extraction, with line coordinate \\(t\\), the finite quotient presentation is\n\\[\n(t,z_1,\\ldots,z_N)\\mapsto\n(\\varepsilon^{-1}t,\\varepsilon^{b_1}z_1,\\ldots,\\varepsilon^{b_N}z_N).\n\\]\nA degree-\\(j\\) function pulls back to \\(t^jF_j(z)\\). The monomial costs\n\\[\nv(t)=N/r,\\qquad v(z_i)=\\delta\n\\]\nrestrict to (4.12). Distinct original degrees have different \\(t\\)-powers, so lowest monomials cannot cancel. Positive rational costs are divisorial up to scaling; at \\(\\delta=0\\) this is the rescaled degree valuation. Positivity on algebra generators gives center \\(o\\).</p><p>The canonical generator has local form\n\\[\nt^{r-1}u(z)\\,dt\\wedge dz_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dz_N,\n\\qquad u(0)\\neq0.\n\\]\nHomogeneity determines the exponent, and nonvanishing on the puncture makes \\(u\\) a unit. Including finite-chart ramification in restricted-value scaling,\n\\[\nA_C(v_\\delta)\n=\\frac Nr+N\\delta+(r-1)\\frac Nr=N+N\\delta.\n\\]</p><p>A leading-term basis in each \\(S_j\\) is compatible with total Taylor order. Colength below \\(q\\) is therefore the number of semigroup indices with\n\\[\nD_0+\\delta|x|&lt;q.\n\\]\nProposition 5 applies. Homogeneity and integration by layers give\n\\[\nn!\\operatorname{Leb}\n\\{\\widetilde K:D_0+\\delta|x|&lt;1\\}\n=\n\\int_{\\widetilde K}e^{-D_0-\\delta|x|}\\,dD_0\\,dx\n=\n\\int_{\\mathbb R_{\\geq0}^N}e^{-f(x)-\\delta|x|}\\,dx.\n\\]\nCombine with density \\(s\\) and the discrepancy formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "The two moment inequalities", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The inequalities (4.16) hold, with \\(\\eta_\\nu\\to0\\), and with zero error on an exact minimizing rational grading.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition 6 and the tests of Lemma 6.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The inequalities (4.16) hold, with \\(\\eta_\\nu\\to0\\), and with zero error on an exact minimizing rational grading.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(F(\\delta)\\) for the right side of (4.14). Then\n\\[\n2N^n\\leq\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)\n\\leq F(0)=N^nsH,\n\\]\ngiving \\(H\\geq2/s\\).</p><p>For\n\\[\nd\\mu_f=H^{-1}e^{-f(x)}\\,dx,\n\\]\npositive homogeneity gives\n\\[\n\\int e^{-tf(x)}\\,dx=t^{-N}H.\n\\]\nDifferentiation, justified by \\(f(x)\\geq c_C|x|\\), yields\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mu_f}f=N,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_{\\mu_f}f^2=N(N+1).\n\\tag{4.17}\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\frac{F'(0)}{F(0)}\n=n-\\mathbb E_{\\mu_f}|x|\n=1+\\mathbb E_{\\mu_f}(f-|x|)\n=\n\\frac{\\mathbb E[(1+B_0)e^{-B_0}]}H.\n\\tag{4.18}\n\\]</p><p>The first two moments of \\(|x|\\) under \\(\\mu_f\\) are uniformly bounded by \\(c_C^{-1}N\\) and \\(c_C^{-2}N(N+1)\\). Since\n\\[\n\\frac{F(\\delta)}{F(0)}\n=(1+\\delta)^n\\mathbb E_{\\mu_f}e^{-\\delta|x|},\n\\]\ntwice differentiating gives\n\\[\n\\sup_{0\\leq\\delta\\leq1}\n\\frac{|F''(\\delta)|}{F(0)}\\leq C'_C.\n\\tag{4.19}\n\\]</p><p>Proposition 3 gives \\(F_\\nu(0)\\to\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)\\). If \\(F'_\\nu(0)/F_\\nu(0)\\) stayed below a fixed negative number along a subsequence, Taylor’s formula and (4.19) would produce fixed small rational \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) and \\(\\rho&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\nF_\\nu(\\delta)\\leq(1-\\rho)F_\\nu(0),\n\\]\ncontradicting \\(F_\\nu(\\delta)\\geq\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)&gt;0\\). Hence the derivative ratio is at least \\(-\\eta_\\nu\\), with \\(\\eta_\\nu\\to0\\). On an exact minimizing grading, its right derivative is nonnegative.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "The exponential bound", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\sup_{N\\geq1}\\ \\sup_{\\substack{a\\in\\mathbb R^N\\\\\\sum_i a_i=0}} \\frac{e^u}{u}\\mathbb E g(u+a\\cdot X) = C_*:=\\frac34e^{4/3}-\\frac e3<2. \\) The supremum over dimensions is approached by …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For \\(y\\in\\mathbb R\\), define\n  \\[\n  g(y)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  1,&amp;y\\leq0,\\\\\n  (1+y)e^{-y},&amp;y&gt;0.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\tag{4.20}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X_1,\\ldots,X_N\\) be independent mean-one exponential random variables.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(u=4/3\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\sup_{N\\geq1}\\ \n\\sup_{\\substack{a\\in\\mathbb R^N\\\\\\sum_i a_i=0}}\n\\frac{e^u}{u}\\mathbb E g(u+a\\cdot X)\n=\nC_*:=\\frac34e^{4/3}-\\frac e3&lt;2.\n\\tag{4.22}\n\\]\nThe supremum over dimensions is approached by\n\\[\na=\\left(2,-\\frac2{N-1},\\ldots,-\\frac2{N-1}\\right)\n\\]\nas \\(N\\to\\infty\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\n\\Phi_N(a)=\\frac{e^u}{u}\\mathbb Eg(u+a\\cdot X),\n\\]\nand let \\(P_N\\) be its supremum under the sum-zero constraint.</p><p>For fixed \\(N\\), take an unbounded sequence \\(a_k\\), and pass to\n\\(a_k/\\|a_k\\|\\to b\\neq0\\). Then \\(\\sum b_i=0\\), and almost surely away from \\(b\\cdot X=0\\),\n\\[\ng(u+a_k\\cdot X)\\to\\mathbf1_{\\{b\\cdot X&lt;0\\}}.\n\\]\nBounded convergence and Lemma 7.1 bound every such limit by\n\\[\nB_*=\\frac{e^u}{u}(1-1/e).\n\\tag{4.28}\n\\]\nThus \\(P_N&gt;B_*\\) forces a bounded maximizing sequence and a finite maximizer. The zero vector has value \\(1+1/u=7/4\\). Every other finite maximizer has the form of Lemma 7.3.</p><p>Appending a zero coefficient makes \\(P_N\\) nondecreasing in \\(N\\), and \\(P_N\\leq e^u/u\\). If its limit exceeds \\(\\max(B_*,7/4)\\), sufficiently large dimensions have nonzero finite maximizers with exceptional coefficient \\(t_N&gt;0\\). On a common probability space their arguments are\n\\[\nY_N=u+t_N\\left(X_1-\\frac1{N-1}\\sum_{i=2}^NX_i\\right).\n\\]\nPass to a subsequence where \\(t_N\\) converges in \\([0,\\infty]\\). If \\(t_N\\to t&lt;\\infty\\), the law of large numbers and bounded convergence give the limit\n\\[\n\\Phi_\\infty(t)\n=\\frac{e^u}{u}\\mathbb Eg(u+t(X_1-1)).\n\\]\nIf \\(t_N\\to\\infty\\), then \\(Y_N/t_N\\to X_1-1\\) almost surely, so the limit is \\(B_*\\). No comparison of \\(t_N\\) with \\(\\sqrt N\\) is needed.</p><p>Direct integration yields\n\\[\n\\Phi_\\infty(t)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\n\\frac{e^t}{u}\n\\left(\\frac{1+u-t}{1+t}+\\frac{t}{(1+t)^2}\\right),\n&amp;0\\leq t\\leq u,\\\\{}[6pt]\n\\displaystyle\n\\frac{e^u}{u}\n\\left(1-e^{-1+u/t}\\frac{t^2}{(1+t)^2}\\right),\n&amp;t\\geq u.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4.29}\n\\]\nFor the second branch split at \\(X_1=1-u/t\\), then translate.</p><p>The first derivative has the sign of\n\\(t(-t^2+ut+u-1)\\), positive on \\([0,u]\\) when \\(u=4/3\\). For the positive quantity subtracted in the second branch, the logarithmic derivative is\n\\[\n-\\frac u{t^2}+\\frac2t-\\frac2{1+t}\n=\\frac{(2-u)t-u}{t^2(1+t)}.\n\\]\nThus the maximum is at \\(t=u/(2-u)=2\\), with value\n\\[\nC_*=\\frac34e^{4/3}-\\frac e3.\n\\]\nIt exceeds \\(7/4\\), and\n\\[\nC_*-B_*=\\frac34e^{1/3}-\\frac e3&gt;0\n\\]\nbecause \\(e^{1/3}&gt;1+1/3\\) and \\(e&lt;3\\). The compactness and limit argument gives the upper bound; taking \\(t_N=2\\) gives the reverse inequality.</p><p>Finally, exponential-series estimates give\n\\[\ne&gt;\\frac{1359}{500},\n\\qquad\ne^{4/3}&lt;\\frac{1897}{500}.\n\\]\nFor the latter, retain terms through degree eight and bound the tail by its first term divided by \\(1-(4/3)/10\\). Hence\n\\[\nC_*&lt;\n\\frac34\\frac{1897}{500}\n-\\frac13\\frac{1359}{500}\n=\\frac{3879}{2000}&lt;2.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Log-concavity and a barycenter half-space bound", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(Z\\) has a positive continuous log-concave density on \\(\\mathbb R\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z\\) be a nonzero mean-zero linear combination of independent mean-one exponential random variables.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(Z\\) has a positive continuous log-concave density on \\(\\mathbb R\\). Adding independent scaled exponentials of either sign preserves these properties. For any such \\(Z\\),\n\\[\n\\frac1e\\leq\\mathbb P(Z\\leq\\mathbb EZ)\\leq1-\\frac1e.\n\\tag{4.24}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A nonzero sum-zero coefficient vector has a positive and a negative coefficient. For \\(a,b&gt;0\\), the difference \\(aE-bE'\\) has density\n\\[\np(x)=\\frac1{a+b}\n\\begin{cases}\ne^{x/b},&amp;x\\leq0,\\\\\ne^{-x/a},&amp;x\\geq0,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nwhich is positive, continuous and log-concave.</p><p>For positive log-concave \\(r\\), let\n\\[\nR(x)=\\int_{-\\infty}^x r(y)\\,dy.\n\\]\nConcavity of \\(\\log r\\) makes \\(r(x-t)/r(x)\\) nondecreasing in \\(x\\), for \\(t\\geq0\\). Hence \\(R/r\\) is nondecreasing and \\((\\log R)'=r/R\\) is nonincreasing. Thus \\(R\\) is log-concave. Similarly\n\\[\nT(x)=\\int_x^\\infty r(y)\\,dy\n\\]\nis log-concave because \\(T/r\\) is nonincreasing.</p><p>Adding \\(cE\\) or \\(-cE\\), \\(c&gt;0\\), to a variable with positive log-concave density \\(p\\) gives respectively\n\\[\n\\frac{e^{-x/c}}c\\int_{-\\infty}^x e^{y/c}p(y)\\,dy,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{e^{x/c}}c\\int_x^\\infty e^{-y/c}p(y)\\,dy.\n\\]\nThe preceding integral argument proves log-concavity; positivity and continuity are immediate. Induction proves the density claims.</p><p>If \\(G\\) is the distribution function of \\(Z\\), both \\(G\\) and \\(1-G\\) are log-concave. Since \\(G(Z)\\) is uniform on \\((0,1)\\), Jensen gives\n\\[\n\\log G(\\mathbb EZ)\n\\geq\\mathbb E\\log G(Z)\n=\\int_0^1\\log t\\,dt=-1.\n\\]\nApply the same argument to \\(1-G\\). All means are finite and both logarithmic expectations equal \\(-1\\), giving (4.24).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Strict single crossing", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\( \\mathbb E k(Z+c_*E)=0, \\) then \\( \\mathbb E k(Z+cE)>0\\quad(c<c_*), \\qquad \\mathbb E k(Z+cE)<0\\quad(c>c_*). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Define \\(h=1-g\\), so\n  \\[\n  h'(y)=ye^{-y}\\mathbf1_{\\{y&gt;0\\}},\n  \\qquad\n  k(y):=h''(y)=(1-y)e^{-y}\\mathbf1_{\\{y&gt;0\\}}\n  \\]\n  almost everywhere.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z\\) have a positive log-concave probability density on \\(\\mathbb R\\), and let \\(E\\) be an independent mean-one exponential.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If\n\\[\n\\mathbb E k(Z+c_*E)=0,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\mathbb E k(Z+cE)&gt;0\\quad(c&lt;c_*),\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E k(Z+cE)&lt;0\\quad(c&gt;c_*).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(p\\) for the density and\n\\[\n\\psi(t)=\\mathbb E k(Z+t)\n=\\int_0^\\infty(1-y)e^{-y}p(y-t)\\,dy.\n\\]\nIt is continuous by continuity of translation in \\(L^1\\) and boundedness of \\(k\\).</p><p>For \\(t_1&lt;t_2\\), log-concavity makes\n\\[\nr(y)=p(y-t_2)/p(y-t_1)\n\\]\nnondecreasing. If \\(\\psi(t_1)=0\\), subtraction of \\(r(1)\\) gives\n\\[\n\\psi(t_2)\n=\n\\int_0^\\infty k(y)p(y-t_1)(r(y)-r(1))\\,dy\\leq0.\n\\]\nThe integrand is nonpositive on both sides of \\(1\\).</p><p>Equality would force \\(r\\) constant on \\((0,\\infty)\\). Thus \\(\\log p\\) has constant increments across a fixed interval on a half-line; concavity forces it to be affine there. Integrability gives\n\\[\np(y-t_1)=Ae^{-\\lambda y}\\quad(y&gt;0),\n\\qquad A,\\lambda&gt;0.\n\\]\nBut then\n\\[\n\\psi(t_1)=A\\int_0^\\infty(1-y)e^{-(1+\\lambda)y}\\,dy\n=\\frac{A\\lambda}{(1+\\lambda)^2}&gt;0,\n\\]\na contradiction. Reversing the comparison gives strict positivity to the left of a zero. Continuity therefore makes \\(\\psi\\) either strictly one-signed everywhere or strictly single-crossing from positive to negative.</p><p>Now set \\(F(c)=\\mathbb E\\psi(cE)\\). For \\(c\\neq0\\), \\(cE\\) has density\n\\[\nq_c(t)=|c|^{-1}e^{-t/c}\\mathbf1_{\\{tc&gt;0\\}}.\n\\]\nFor \\(c_1&lt;c_2\\) of the same sign,\n\\[\n\\frac{q_{c_2}(t)}{q_{c_1}(t)}\n=\n\\frac{|c_1|}{|c_2|}\n\\exp\\bigl(t(1/c_1-1/c_2)\\bigr)\n\\]\nis strictly increasing on the common support. Subtraction of its value at the crossing proves the required signs within that half-line.</p><p>If \\(c_*&gt;0\\), the crossing of \\(\\psi\\) lies in \\((0,\\infty)\\), so \\(\\psi&gt;0\\) on \\((-\\infty,0]\\), and \\(F(c)&gt;0\\) for \\(c\\leq0\\). The negative case is analogous. At \\(c_*=0\\), use \\(F(0)=\\psi(0)\\) and the signs of \\(\\psi\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "Finite critical points", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every nonzero finite local maximum has, after permutation, the form \\( a=\\left(t,-\\frac t{N-1},\\ldots,-\\frac t{N-1}\\right), \\qquad t>0. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u&gt;0\\), \\(N\\geq2\\), and retain \\(g,h,k\\) above.</p><p class=\"li\">Consider\n  \\[\n  a\\longmapsto\\mathbb E g\\!\\left(u+\\sum_{i=1}^Na_iX_i\\right)\n  \\quad\\text{on}\\quad \\sum_i a_i=0.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every nonzero finite local maximum has, after permutation, the form\n\\[\na=\\left(t,-\\frac t{N-1},\\ldots,-\\frac t{N-1}\\right),\n\\qquad t&gt;0.\n\\tag{4.25}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Equivalently consider a local minimum of \\(\\mathbb Eh(Y)\\), with\n\\(Y=u+\\sum_i a_iX_i\\). Lemma 7.1 gives a continuous positive log-concave density at a nonzero coefficient vector. Differentiation twice is justified by boundedness of \\(h'\\), its global Lipschitz property, finite second moments and absence of an atom at \\(Y=0\\).</p><p>Stationarity in direction \\(e_i-e_j\\) gives\n\\[\n0=\\mathbb E[(X_i-X_j)h'(Y)]\n=(a_i-a_j)\\mathbb E[X_iX_jk(Y)].\n\\tag{4.26}\n\\]\nFor the second identity condition on the other variables and \\(S=X_i+X_j\\). Given \\(S=s\\), \\(X_i\\) is uniform on \\([0,s]\\). Integration by parts with \\(x(s-x)\\) gives the formula.</p><p>Weighting by \\(X_iX_j\\) changes those two exponential laws to independent \\(\\operatorname{Gamma}(2,1)\\) laws. Since \\(\\mathbb EX_iX_j=1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[X_iX_jk(Y)]\n=\\mathbb Ek(Y+a_iE'+a_jE'').\n\\tag{4.27}\n\\]\nIf three coefficient values occurred, fix an index at one value and choose indices at the other two. Equations (4.26)–(4.27) would give distinct zeros of\n\\[\nc\\longmapsto\\mathbb Ek(Y+a_iE'+cE''),\n\\]\ncontradicting Lemma 7.2. Thus there are at most two values.</p><p>Write them \\(b&lt;c\\). If \\(c\\) occurred at two indices \\(i\\neq j\\), a high-low pair gives a zero at \\(b\\) of\n\\[\nd\\longmapsto\\mathbb Ek(Y+cE'+dE'').\n\\]\nStrict crossing makes its value at \\(c\\) negative, so\n\\(\\mathbb E[X_iX_jk(Y)]&lt;0\\). Since equal coefficients make \\(Y\\) depend on this pair only through \\(S\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[(X_i-X_j)^2\\mid S]=S^2/3,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E[X_iX_j\\mid S]=S^2/6.\n\\]\nThe Hessian in the splitting direction is therefore negative, contrary to a minimum. The higher value occurs once, and sum zero gives (4.25).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Canonical interior point", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every sufficiently far grading, \\( c_0\\in\\operatorname{int}K. \\) Equivalently, \\(f(\\mathbf1)<N\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Propositions 5 and 6: a fixed cone with\n  \\(\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)\\geq2N^n\\), sufficiently fast gradings in the range \\(m\\geq r/N\\), the jet cone \\(K\\), and \\(c_0=(r,\\mathbf1)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently far grading,\n\\[\nc_0\\in\\operatorname{int}K.\n\\tag{4.30}\n\\]\nEquivalently, \\(f(\\mathbf1)&lt;N\\). For an exact minimizing rational grading, the conclusion holds without passage to a limit.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(f(\\mathbf1)\\geq N\\). Choose a subgradient \\(p\\) of \\(f\\) at the interior orthant point \\(\\mathbf1\\). Positive homogeneity gives\n\\[\np\\cdot\\mathbf1=f(\\mathbf1),\n\\qquad f(x)\\geq p\\cdot x.\n\\]\nThus \\(B_0(x)\\geq(p-\\mathbf1)\\cdot x\\), whose coefficient sum is nonnegative. Subtracting its mean coefficient gives a sum-zero vector \\(a\\) with\n\\[\nB_0(x)\\geq a\\cdot x.\n\\tag{4.31}\n\\]</p><p>For every \\(v\\in\\mathbb R\\) and \\(u&gt;0\\), elementary differentiation gives\n\\[\n\\sup_{b\\geq v}\n\\left(1+\\frac{1+b}{u}\\right)e^{-b}\n=\\frac{e^u}{u}g(u+v).\n\\tag{4.21}\n\\]\nIndeed, the derivative before maximization is\n\\(-(u+b)e^{-b}/u\\), so the maximizer is \\(-u\\) if \\(v\\leq-u\\), and \\(v\\) otherwise.</p><p>With \\(u=4/3\\), Propositions 6 and 7 now imply\n\\[\n(1-\\eta_\\nu/u)H\n\\leq\n\\mathbb E\\left[\\left(1+\\frac{1+B_0(X)}u\\right)e^{-B_0(X)}\\right]\n\\leq\\frac{e^u}{u}\\mathbb Eg(u+a\\cdot X)\n\\leq C_*&lt;2.\n\\]\nAll expectations are finite by \\(f(x)\\geq c_C|x|\\). But \\(H\\geq2/s\\geq2\\), a contradiction for sufficiently small \\(\\eta_\\nu\\). On an exact minimizing grading, \\(\\eta_\\nu=0\\) gives the contradiction immediately.</p><p>The scaled coordinates of \\(c_0\\) are \\((N,\\mathbf1)\\), with \\(\\mathbf1\\) interior to the exponent orthant. Hence \\(f(\\mathbf1)&lt;N\\) is exactly interiority.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Adjoint lattice points", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( (j,\\alpha)\\in\\Lambda,\\qquad (j,\\alpha)+c_0\\in\\operatorname{int}K \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (j,\\alpha)\\in\\Gamma. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B\\) be a smooth effective proper complex orbifold of dimension \\(N\\), with projective coarse space.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be an ample orbifold line bundle satisfying \\(-K_B=rL\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At a chosen point let the stabilizer be \\(\\mu_m\\), with faithful fiber character \\(1\\) on \\(L\\), and equivariant algebraic étale parameters \\(z_i\\) of characters \\(b_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Gamma,\\Lambda,K,c_0=(r,\\mathbf1)\\) be the original-degree jet data of Proposition 5. In particular, \\(K\\) is full-dimensional, has bounded original-degree slices, projects onto the exponent orthant, and contains the positive constant ray.</p><p class=\"li\">Unlike Proposition 8, do not assume \\(c_0\\in\\operatorname{int}K\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n(j,\\alpha)\\in\\Lambda,\\qquad\n(j,\\alpha)+c_0\\in\\operatorname{int}K\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n(j,\\alpha)\\in\\Gamma.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The coordinate inequalities of \\(K\\) give \\(\\alpha_i+1&gt;0\\), so \\(\\alpha_i\\geq0\\). Positivity of original degree on \\(K\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) gives \\(j+r&gt;0\\). Choose rational\n\\[\n0&lt;j_0&lt;j+r,\\qquad\np=(j_0,\\alpha+\\mathbf1)\\in\\operatorname{int}K.\n\\]</p><p>Choose finitely many section initials whose cone contains \\(p\\) in its interior. This follows by surrounding \\(p\\) by a small simplex inside \\(K\\), approximating its vertices by points of \\(\\operatorname{cone}(\\Gamma)\\), and collecting the finite lists used.</p><p>Choose positive primitive integral weights \\(u_1,\\ldots,u_N\\) for which every section in this finite list has its prescribed initial as a single scalar-weight initial. For the finite-order justification, take \\(d\\) exceeding their initial total degrees and \\(|\\alpha|\\). Weights close to a common positive value preserve total-degree comparisons through \\(d\\) and put higher degrees above the relevant initials. Successive small rational perturbations realize lexicographic comparisons. Include all monomials preceding \\(\\alpha\\), then clear denominators. Thus\n\\[\n\\gamma\\text{ precedes }\\alpha\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nu\\cdot\\gamma&lt;u\\cdot\\alpha.\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\np_0=u\\cdot\\alpha,\\qquad\nU=u\\cdot(\\alpha+\\mathbf1)=p_0+\\sum_i u_i.\n\\]</p><p>Intersect the finite cone with original degree \\(j_0\\) and scalar cost \\(U\\). Choose rational points surrounding \\(p\\) in this plane and rational nonnegative expressions in the chosen generators. Clearing denominators and multiplying sections yields \\(a&gt;0\\) and sections \\(s_k\\) of \\(aj_0L\\), with single initials \\(z^{\\beta^{(k)}}\\), such that\n\\[\nu\\cdot\\beta^{(k)}=aU,\n\\qquad\n\\alpha+\\mathbf1\\in\n\\operatorname{relint}\\operatorname{conv}\n\\{\\beta^{(1)}/a,\\ldots,\\beta^{(t)}/a\\}\n\\subset\\{x:u\\cdot x=U\\}.\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nThe relative interior contains a neighborhood in the entire displayed plane.</p><p>For \\(k\\geq0\\), let \\(I_k\\) be the local monomial ideal of \\(u\\)-weight at least \\(k\\). Each contains a power of the maximal ideal, so its finite quotient descends equivariantly to the chosen residual gerbe and extends by the unit ideal off the center. Use finite-group translates on larger charts. The ideals form a global multiplicative filtration.</p><p>The weighted Rees algebra\n\\[\n\\mathcal A=\\bigoplus_{k\\geq0}I_kq^k\n\\]\nis locally generated by \\(z_iq^\\ell\\), \\(1\\leq\\ell\\leq u_i\\). Define\n\\[\n\\pi:\\mathcal Z=\n[(\\operatorname{Spec}_B\\mathcal A\\setminus V(\\mathcal A_+))/\\mathbb G_m]\n\\longrightarrow B.\n\\]\nThis is the identity away from the center. Put \\(x_i=z_iq^{u_i}\\). Their nonvanishing loci cover the irrelevant open because\n\\[\n(z_iq^\\ell)^{u_i}=x_i^\\ell z_i^{u_i-\\ell}.\n\\]\nOn \\(x_i\\neq0\\), the regular ratio\n\\(t=(z_iq^{u_i-1})/x_i\\) satisfies \\(z_j=t^{u_j}x_j\\). Before the original cyclic quotient, the model is\n\\[\n[(\\mathbb A^{N+1}_{x,t}\\setminus\\{x_1=\\cdots=x_N=0\\})/\\mathbb G_m],\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n\\rho\\cdot(x_i,t)=(\\rho^{u_i}x_i,\\rho^{-1}t).\n\\]\nThe original \\(\\mu_m\\) acts on \\(x_i\\) by \\(b_i\\) and trivially on \\(t\\). This is a smooth stack with finite stabilizers. Its exceptional Cartier divisor is\n\\[\nE=\\{t=0\\}=[\\mathbb P(u_1,\\ldots,u_N)/\\mu_m],\n\\]\nretaining generic inertia. A divisible \\(\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal Z}(-qE)\\) descends to the relatively ample tautological line bundle of the coarse weighted blowup, so the coarse space is projective.</p><p>The Jacobian gives\n\\[\nK_{\\mathcal Z}+E=\\pi^*K_B+\\left(\\sum_i u_i\\right)E.\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nP_0=\\pi^*(jL)-p_0E,\n\\qquad\nH_0=\\pi^*(aj_0L)-aUE.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nP_0-(K_{\\mathcal Z}+E)-\\frac1aH_0\n=(j+r-j_0)\\pi^*L,\n\\tag{5.6}\n\\]\nwhich is nef and big. The sections \\(s_k\\), after removing common exceptional order, define sections of \\(H_0\\) with base ideal \\(\\mathfrak b\\), not identically zero on \\(E\\).</p><p>The congruence \\((j,\\alpha)\\in\\Lambda\\) makes \\(z^\\alpha\\) a section of \\(P_0|_E\\), of scalar degree \\(p_0\\). Claim:\n\\[\nz^\\alpha\\in\nH^0\\!\\left(E,P_0|_E\\otimes\n\\mathcal J_E((\\mathfrak b|_E)^{1/a})\\right).\n\\tag{5.7}\n\\]\nOn the chart \\(x_k\\neq0\\), use the smooth finite atlas setting \\(x_k=1\\), with finite group\n\\[\nH_k=\\{(\\rho,\\zeta)\\in\\mathbb G_m\\times\\mu_m:\n\\rho^{u_k}\\zeta^{b_k}=1\\}.\n\\]\nIt acts on remaining coordinates by \\(x_i\\mapsto\\rho^{u_i}\\zeta^{b_i}x_i\\), retaining any ineffective generic inertia. The line characters are \\(\\rho^{p_0}\\zeta^j\\) and \\(\\rho^{aU}\\zeta^{aj_0}\\). The congruence gives the required character for \\(x_{\\mathrm{rem}}^{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{rem}}}\\).</p><p>Dropping coordinate \\(k\\) is an affine isomorphism from \\(u\\cdot x=U\\). Equation (5.4) places \\(\\alpha_{\\mathrm{rem}}+\\mathbf1\\) in the ordinary interior of the projected scaled Newton polyhedron. Howald’s formula [17] therefore proves (5.7) on each atlas, and equivariance descends it.</p><p>Take a projective log resolution\n\\(\\sigma:\\mathcal Y\\to\\mathcal Z\\) of \\(E\\) and \\(\\mathfrak b\\), isomorphic generically on \\(E\\). Functorial resolutions in smooth étale charts descend [23]. Arrange simple normal crossings and that\n\\(\\tau:\\widetilde E\\to E\\) resolves the restricted ideal. Write\n\\[\n\\mathfrak b\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal Y}=\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal Y}(-G_0).\n\\]\nThe divisor \\(G_0\\) does not contain \\(\\widetilde E\\), and\n\\(\\sigma^*H_0-G_0\\) is globally generated.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nA_{\\mathcal Y}\n=K_{\\mathcal Y}\n+\\sigma^*(P_0-K_{\\mathcal Z}-E)\n-\\lfloor G_0/a\\rfloor.\n\\]\nIts difference from \\(K_{\\mathcal Y}+\\{G_0/a\\}\\) is\n\\[\n(j+r-j_0)\\sigma^*\\pi^*L\n+\\frac1a(\\sigma^*H_0-G_0),\n\\tag{5.8}\n\\]\nnef and big. Lemma 9.1 gives \\(H^1(\\mathcal Y,A_{\\mathcal Y})=0\\), hence\n\\[\nH^0(\\mathcal Y,A_{\\mathcal Y}+\\widetilde E)\n\\twoheadrightarrow\nH^0(\\widetilde E,(A_{\\mathcal Y}+\\widetilde E)|_{\\widetilde E}).\n\\tag{5.9}\n\\]\nAdjunction identifies the restricted line bundle with\n\\[\n\\tau^*(P_0|_E)+K_{\\widetilde E}-\\tau^*K_E\n-\\lfloor(G_0|_{\\widetilde E})/a\\rfloor.\n\\tag{5.10}\n\\]\nRound-down commutes with restriction because of simple normal crossings; distinct components do not share a divisor on \\(\\widetilde E\\). Equation (5.7) supplies the pullback of \\(z^\\alpha\\) as a section of (5.10), which lifts by (5.9).</p><p>The lifting bundle differs from \\(\\sigma^*P_0\\) by\n\\[\nK_{\\mathcal Y}-\\sigma^*K_{\\mathcal Z}\n-\\sigma^*E+\\widetilde E-\\lfloor G_0/a\\rfloor.\n\\tag{5.11}\n\\]\nThe part before the last term is effective and exceptional: a smooth blowup center of codimension \\(c\\geq2\\) contributes \\(c-1\\), or \\(c-2\\) if contained in the strict transform of \\(E\\), together with pullbacks of previous effective exceptional terms. The lifted rational section has no pole along any nonexceptional prime, so pushes down regularly to \\(P_0\\). Its restriction to integral \\(E\\) is \\(z^\\alpha\\).</p><p>Since \\(p_0\\geq0\\), \\(P_0\\subset\\pi^*(jL)\\), and \\(\\pi_*\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal Z}=\\mathcal O_B\\) by normal extension. Pushdown gives a section of \\(jL\\) with single \\(u\\)-initial \\(z^\\alpha\\). Equation (5.3) excludes every monomial preceding \\(\\alpha\\) in the original order. Thus \\((j,\\alpha)\\in\\Gamma\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Orbifold vanishing", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( H^q(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal X}(D))=0 \\qquad(q>0). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal X\\) be a smooth proper Deligne–Mumford stack over \\(\\mathbb C\\), with trivial generic stabilizer and projective coarse space.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Theta\\) be an effective rational divisor with simple normal crossing support and coefficients less than one.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D\\) be Cartier.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(D-(K_{\\mathcal X}+\\Theta)\\) is nef and big, with these notions understood on the projective coarse space.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nH^q(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal X}(D))=0\n\\qquad(q&gt;0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(q:\\mathcal X\\to X\\) be the coarse morphism. For a prime divisor \\(B\\subset X\\), let \\(e_B\\) be the generic stabilizer order above it. A rational divisor \\(A\\) has a coarse divisor \\(A_c\\) characterized in codimension one by \\(q^*A_c=A\\). Locally \\(t=z^{e_B}\\), so\n\\[\nK_{\\mathcal X}=q^*(K_X+R),\n\\qquad\nR=\\sum_B(1-1/e_B)B.\n\\]</p><p>Since coefficients of \\(D_c\\) are integral multiples of \\(1/e_B\\),\n\\[\n\\Delta=R+\\Theta_c-\\{D_c\\}\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nis effective. The pair \\((X,\\Delta)\\) is klt: on a smooth finite quotient uniformizer its log pullback is the pullback of \\(\\Theta\\) minus an effective divisor, hence bounded above by the simple normal crossing boundary of coefficients less than one. The finite-map discrepancy formula transfers positivity downstairs.</p><p>Normal extension from codimension one gives\n\\[\nq_*\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal X}(D)=\\mathcal O_X(\\lfloor D_c\\rfloor).\n\\]\nOn quotient charts, invariant sections of an invertible module extend across codimension at least two; their codimension-one orders are exactly \\(\\lfloor D_c\\rfloor\\). Exactness of invariants in characteristic zero also gives equality of cohomology.</p><p>The coarse space has quotient singularities and is locally \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factorial. Thus \\(\\lfloor D_c\\rfloor\\) is an integral \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier Weil divisor, and\n\\[\n\\lfloor D_c\\rfloor-(K_X+\\Delta)\n=D_c-(K_X+R+\\Theta_c)\n\\]\nis the descended nef and big class. Kawamata–Viehweg vanishing for this klt pair [12] proves the result.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "The saturated jet semigroup", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(K\\) is rational polyhedral.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the orbifold jet data and adjoint implication of Proposition 9.</p><p class=\"li\">Add the assumption \\(c_0\\in\\operatorname{int}K\\cap\\Lambda\\), as supplied by Proposition 8 in the volume-threshold setting.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(K\\) is rational polyhedral.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every facet has a primitive nonnegative support form \\(\\eta\\in\\Lambda^*\\) with \\(\\eta(c_0)=1\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. One has\n   \\[\n   \\Gamma=K\\cap\\Lambda,\n   \\qquad\n   \\operatorname{int}K\\cap\\Lambda=c_0+\\Gamma.\n   \\tag{5.12}\n   \\]\nThus \\(\\Gamma\\) is a finitely generated saturated affine semigroup with group \\(\\Lambda\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(\\phi=(r/N)f\\), so its epigraph uses original degree \\(j\\). On the positive exponent orthant, \\(\\phi\\) is finite, convex and locally Lipschitz.</p><p>At a differentiability point \\(x\\), put \\(y=(\\phi(x),x)\\). Homogeneity gives\n\\(\\phi(x)=\\nabla\\phi(x)\\cdot x\\), and the unique supporting normal ray is generated by\n\\[\n\\eta(j',x')=j'-\\nabla\\phi(x)\\cdot x'.\n\\]\nSince \\(c_0\\) is interior, \\(\\eta(c_0)&gt;0\\).</p><p>Suppose this ray is irrational relative to \\(\\Lambda\\), or its primitive integral generator has height greater than one on \\(c_0\\). Then there is \\(w\\in\\Lambda\\) with\n\\[\n-\\eta(c_0)&lt;\\eta(w)&lt;0.\n\\tag{5.13}\n\\]\nFor an integral form of height at least two, choose value \\(-1\\). For an irrational ray, \\(\\eta(\\Lambda)\\) is dense: otherwise it is discrete, making a positive multiple primitive integral.</p><p>Recurrence of multiples of \\(y\\) in \\(\\mathbb R^{N+1}/\\Lambda\\) gives integers \\(k_\\nu\\to\\infty\\) and \\(\\ell_\\nu\\in\\Lambda\\) with\n\\[\ne_\\nu=\\ell_\\nu-k_\\nu y\\to0.\n\\tag{5.14}\n\\]\nIf return times are bounded, an exact period and its multiples give the same conclusion.</p><p>The points \\(\\ell_\\nu+w\\) lie outside \\(K\\) for large \\(\\nu\\). But\n\\[\n\\ell_\\nu+w+c_0\\in\\operatorname{int}K.\n\\tag{5.15}\n\\]\nIndeed, set \\(v=w+c_0\\), so \\(\\eta(v)&gt;0\\). Differentiability gives, uniformly for nearby \\(v'\\),\n\\[\n\\phi(x+tv'_x)=\\phi(x)+t\\nabla\\phi(x)\\cdot v'_x+o(t).\n\\]\nFor small \\(t&gt;0\\), \\(y+tv'\\) lies strictly above the epigraph boundary and retains positive exponents. Apply this with \\(t=1/k_\\nu\\), \\(v'=v+e_\\nu\\), then scale. Proposition 9 would force \\(\\ell_\\nu+w\\in\\Gamma\\subset K\\), a contradiction. Thus every support at a differentiability point is primitive integral of height one.</p><p>Choose \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) with the \\(\\varepsilon\\)-ball about \\(c_0\\) contained in \\(K\\). Any nonnegative support with height one has norm at most \\(1/\\varepsilon\\). Only finitely many integral forms occur; call them \\(\\eta_1,\\ldots,\\eta_t\\).</p><p>These forms and \\(x_i\\geq0\\) define \\(K\\). At any positive \\(x_0\\), choose differentiability points \\(x_\\nu\\to x_0\\). Local Lipschitz continuity bounds gradients, and\n\\[\n\\phi(x_\\nu)+\\nabla\\phi(x_\\nu)\\cdot(x_0-x_\\nu)\\to\\phi(x_0).\n\\]\nHence the finite supports recover the epigraph on the positive orthant. If \\(K'\\) is their intersection with the coordinate half-spaces, then \\(K\\subset K'\\). For \\(q\\in K'\\), \\(q+tc_0\\) has positive exponents and lies in \\(K\\) for \\(t&gt;0\\); closedness gives \\(q\\in K\\). Thus \\(K=K'\\).</p><p>Each coordinate form is primitive on \\(\\Lambda\\), since \\(x=e_i\\) admits a compatible degree, and has height one on \\(c_0\\). Every facet comes from an irredundant defining sublist, proving the facet assertion.</p><p>For \\(q\\in K\\cap\\Lambda\\), translating a small ball about \\(c_0\\) by \\(q\\) gives \\(q+c_0\\in\\operatorname{int}K\\), so Proposition 9 gives \\(q\\in\\Gamma\\). Conversely \\(\\Gamma\\subset K\\cap\\Lambda\\).</p><p>For \\(q\\in\\operatorname{int}K\\cap\\Lambda\\), every primitive facet form has positive integral value, hence at least one. Subtracting \\(c_0\\) preserves every facet inequality, so \\(q-c_0\\in K\\cap\\Lambda=\\Gamma\\). The reverse inclusion follows by ball translation.</p><p>Finally, triangulate the rational polyhedral cone into rational simplicial cones. Each lattice point is a nonnegative integral combination of ray generators plus a lattice point in a bounded half-open parallelepiped. There are finitely many remainders, proving finite generation. If a positive multiple of \\(q\\in\\Lambda\\) belongs to \\(K\\cap\\Lambda\\), homogeneity puts \\(q\\) there as well. The generated group is \\(\\Lambda\\) by Proposition 5.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "The Taylor graded ring", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The algebra \\(\\bar S\\) is finitely generated, normal, Cohen–Macaulay and Gorenstein, with canonical generator of bidegree \\((r,N)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix one grading and point supplied by Proposition 10.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n=N+1\\), \\(N\\geq4\\), and let \\(S=\\mathbb C[C]\\) be the singular positively graded normal Gorenstein cone ring, with canonical generator of original degree \\(r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma=K\\cap\\Lambda,\\qquad\n  \\operatorname{int}K\\cap\\Lambda=c_0+\\Gamma,\n  \\qquad c_0=(r,\\mathbf1).\n  \\tag{6.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For original-homogeneous sections, let \\(T\\) be total Taylor order, extended by direct sums in original degree. Write\n  \\[\n  \\bar S=\\operatorname{gr}_T S.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Bidegree means original degree and \\(T\\)-degree, in that order.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The algebra \\(\\bar S\\) is finitely generated, normal, Cohen–Macaulay and Gorenstein, with canonical generator of bidegree \\((r,N)\\).</p><p>There is \\(v\\in S_m\\) with \\(T(v)=0\\) and Taylor initial \\(\\bar v=\\tau^m\\), where \\(\\tau\\) records original degree, such that\n\\[\n\\bar S[\\bar v^{-1}]\n\\simeq\\mathbb C[\\bar v,\\bar v^{-1},u_1,\\ldots,u_N],\n\\qquad T(u_i)=1,\\quad T(\\bar v)=0.\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\nFor\n\\[\nD=\\bar S/(\\bar v),\n\\]\nthe ring \\(D\\) is equidimensional Cohen–Macaulay Gorenstein of dimension \\(N\\), and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hilb}_T D(z)=(1-z)^{-N},\n\\qquad\n\\deg\\omega_D=(r-m,N).\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\nIts \\(T\\)-degree-zero piece is \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p><p>A bihomogeneous vector-space basis of \\(D\\) lifts to original-homogeneous sections \\(e_i\\in S\\), with \\(T_i=T(e_i)\\), for which\n\\[\n\\{v^ae_i:a\\in\\mathbb N\\}\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\nis a vector-space basis of \\(S\\), compatible with original degree and Taylor order. Its unique vector with \\(a+T_i=0\\) is \\(1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The ring \\(\\bar S\\) is the subalgebra of \\(\\mathbb C[\\tau,z_1,\\ldots,z_N]\\) formed by homogeneous Taylor forms \\(\\tau^jP(z)\\). Its refined monomial initial algebra is \\(\\mathbb C[\\Gamma]\\).</p><p>By Proposition 10, \\(\\Gamma\\) is finitely generated and normal. Hochster’s theorem makes \\(\\mathbb C[\\Gamma]\\) Cohen–Macaulay. The normal-semigroup canonical-module formula identifies its canonical ideal with interior lattice monomials [16, 47]. The interior translation in (6.1) makes that ideal principal, generated in multidegree \\((r,\\mathbf1)\\), hence bidegree \\((r,N)\\).</p><p>Choose finite semigroup generators and bihomogeneous lifts in \\(\\bar S\\). Degreewise subduction terminates: a fixed original degree has finitely many Taylor orders, and each order finitely many monomials. Thus these lifts generate \\(\\bar S\\).</p><p>Choose positive integral scalar costs on the \\(z_i\\) preserving the selected initials of the finite lifts. The scalar initial algebra contains \\(\\mathbb C[\\Gamma]\\), and dimensions agree in every original bidegree, so equality holds. Generator lifting gives a finite-type extended Rees algebra \\(R\\), flat over its parameter \\(\\lambda\\), with central fiber \\(\\mathbb C[\\Gamma]\\) and nonzero fibers \\(\\bar S\\).</p><p>It retains original degree, Taylor degree and scalar grading, with scalar weight \\(-1\\) on \\(\\lambda\\). A large multiple of original degree minus scalar weight gives a positive grading. At its vertex, the local ring \\(A\\) has normal Gorenstein quotient \\(A/(\\lambda)\\). Lifting through the nonzerodivisor \\(\\lambda\\) makes \\(A\\) Cohen–Macaulay and Gorenstein [48, Tag 0BJJ].</p><p>It is normal by \\(R_1+S_2\\). For a height-one prime \\(P\\), if \\(\\lambda\\in P\\), reducedness makes \\(A_P/(\\lambda)\\) a field, hence \\(A_P\\) regular. Otherwise take \\(Q\\) minimal over \\((P,\\lambda)\\). The dimension formula and principal ideal theorem give \\(\\operatorname{ht}Q\\leq2\\). The normal ring \\(A_Q/(\\lambda)\\), of dimension at most one, is regular; lifting parameters with \\(\\lambda\\) makes \\(A_Q\\), hence \\(A_P\\), regular.</p><p>The normal and Gorenstein loci of \\(\\operatorname{Spec}R\\) are open, grading-invariant and contain the vertex. Every orbit contracts there, so both loci are all of \\(\\operatorname{Spec}R\\). Normality of the generic fiber descends under the faithfully flat field extension, proving normality of \\(\\bar S\\); quotient by \\(\\lambda-1\\) proves its Cohen–Macaulay and Gorenstein properties.</p><p>Adjunction tracks canonical bidegree. The parameter has original and Taylor degrees zero. Lift the central canonical generator, homogeneous also for scalar grading. Its cokernel equals its product by \\(\\lambda\\), hence vanishes by graded Nakayama in the positive combined grading. Restriction to \\(\\lambda=1\\) gives canonical bidegree \\((r,N)\\).</p><p>The index \\((m,0)\\) lies on the constant ray and belongs to \\(\\Lambda\\), so saturation supplies \\(v\\) with initial \\(\\tau^m\\). Eventual jet separation shows that after inverting \\(\\tau^m\\), every compatible monomial occurs. Choose integer representatives \\(b_i\\), and set \\(u_i=\\tau^{b_i}z_i\\) in the localization. The congruence expresses every allowed monomial uniquely as a Laurent power of \\(\\bar v\\) times a monomial in the \\(u_i\\), proving (6.3).</p><p>For fixed Taylor degree \\(q\\), the corresponding \\(\\bar S\\)-piece is a finite torsion-free \\(\\mathbb C[\\bar v]\\)-module. Finiteness follows because, in each original-degree residue class modulo \\(m\\), multiplication by \\(\\bar v\\) gives an increasing sequence of subspaces of the finite-dimensional space of compatible degree-\\(q\\) forms, eventually full by jet separation. It is free over the PID, of rank\n\\[\n\\binom{q+N-1}{N-1}\n\\]\nby localization. Reduction modulo \\(\\bar v\\) gives the Hilbert series.</p><p>The nonzerodivisor quotient \\(D\\) is equidimensional Cohen–Macaulay of dimension \\(N\\). Adjunction subtracts \\((m,0)\\) from the canonical degree. Its degree-zero piece is \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p><p>Lift a bihomogeneous basis of \\(D\\) to \\(\\bar S\\). Reduction modulo \\(\\bar v\\), descending original degree, proves generation over \\(\\mathbb C[\\bar v]\\); successive removal of the smallest power proves independence. Lift again through the Taylor filtration to \\(e_i\\in S\\). Terminating cancellation proves (6.5). Choose \\(e_i=1\\) for the unique degree-zero vector; all other \\(T_i\\) are positive.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Rees discrepancy comparison", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The Rees algebra is finite type.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S=\\mathbb C[C]\\) be the positively graded normal Gorenstein cone ring above, with canonical form \\(\\Omega_C\\) of original degree \\(r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\) be a nonnegative decreasing multiplicative integral filtration, with \\(F_pS=S\\) for \\(p\\leq0\\), respecting original degree and of finite length in each original homogeneous piece.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the extended Rees convention\n  \\[\n  R_F=\\sum_{p\\in\\mathbb Z}\\lambda^{-p}F_pS\n  \\subset S[\\lambda,\\lambda^{-1}].\n  \\tag{6.6}\n  \\]\n  The action has weight zero on \\(S\\), weight \\(-1\\) on \\(\\lambda\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\operatorname{gr}_FS\\) is finitely generated, Cohen–Macaulay and Gorenstein, with canonical generator of bidegree \\((r,Q)\\), and that\n  \\(\\mathcal X=\\operatorname{Spec}R_F\\) is normal and Gorenstein.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The Rees algebra is finite type. Its absolute canonical module has generator of bidegree \\((r,Q-1)\\), which on \\(\\lambda\\neq0\\) is\n\\[\n\\Omega_{\\mathcal X}\n=c\\lambda^{-Q}\\Omega_C\\wedge d\\lambda,\n\\qquad c\\in\\mathbb C^*.\n\\tag{6.7}\n\\]\nFor a quasi-monomial valuation \\(V\\) centered on \\(\\mathcal X\\) over \\(\\lambda=0\\), put\n\\(\\ell=V(\\lambda)&gt;0\\) and \\(w=V|_{K(C)}\\). If \\(w\\) is centered at \\(o\\), then\n\\[\nA_C(w)\\leq A_{\\mathcal X}(V)+(Q-1)\\ell.\n\\tag{6.8}\n\\]\nThe same inequality holds whenever the discrepancies involved are finite and defined by valuation retractions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lift homogeneous generators of \\(\\operatorname{gr}_FS\\) to \\(s_i\\in S\\), of filtration orders \\(p_i\\). Cancellation of initials terminates within each original degree and shows that \\(R_F\\) is generated by \\(\\lambda\\) and \\(\\lambda^{-p_i}s_i\\). A sufficiently large multiple of original degree minus filtration degree makes these generators positive, with \\(\\deg\\lambda=1\\).</p><p>Cartier adjunction for the nonzerodivisor \\(\\lambda\\) [48, Tag 0B4A] identifies canonical reduction with the central canonical module, with the shift from \\(d\\lambda\\). A lifted central generator has bidegree \\((r,Q-1)\\). Its cokernel equals its product by \\(\\lambda\\); boundedness below in the positive grading makes it zero. Torsion-freeness gives a free generator.</p><p>On \\(\\lambda\\neq0\\), its ratio to \\(\\Omega_C\\wedge d\\lambda\\) is a unit of \\(S[\\lambda,\\lambda^{-1}]\\). Units of the positive domain \\(S\\) are constants, so the ratio is \\(c\\lambda^a\\). Comparing filtration weights gives \\(a=-Q\\).</p><p>On a common smooth birational model, the top-form identity gives\n\\[\nA_{C\\times\\mathbb A^1}(V)\n=A_{\\mathcal X}(V)+Q\\ell.\n\\tag{6.9}\n\\]\nNo properness of the Rees morphism is required.</p><p>Also\n\\[\nA_{C\\times\\mathbb A^1}(V)\\geq A_C(w)+\\ell.\n\\tag{6.10}\n\\]\nTake a smooth proper birational log model used to retract \\(w\\), multiply it by the parameter line, and include \\(\\lambda=0\\) among the normal crossing divisors. The monomial retraction of \\(V\\) has discrepancy equal to that of the corresponding retraction of \\(w\\), plus \\(\\ell\\). Log discrepancy dominates every retraction value; taking the supremum proves (6.10). Combining the two formulas proves (6.8).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "The two-filtration structure", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The ring \\(D\\) is reduced.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(S,\\bar S,D,v,e_i,T_i\\) from Proposition 11, with \\(n=N+1\\), \\(N\\geq4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n  \\[\n  \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)\\geq2N^n.\n  \\tag{6.2}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define the second filtration by assigning the basis vectors the costs\n  \\[\n  W(v^ae_i)=a+T_i\n  \\tag{6.12}\n  \\]\n  and taking spans of vectors of cost at least the cutoff.</p><p class=\"li\">Equivalently, with \\(T_qS=S\\) for \\(q\\leq0\\),\n  \\[\n  W_pS=\\sum_{a=0}^pv^aT_{p-a}S\n  \\qquad(p\\geq0).\n  \\tag{6.13}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The ring \\(D\\) is reduced. The filtration \\(W\\) is multiplicative and\n\\[\nG=\\operatorname{gr}_WS\\simeq D[v],\n\\qquad W(v)=1,\\quad W|_D=T,\n\\tag{6.14}\n\\]\nwhere \\(v\\) is a regular polynomial variable of original degree \\(m\\). Moreover\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hilb}_WG(z)=(1-z)^{-n},\n\\qquad\n\\deg\\omega_G=(r,n).\n\\tag{6.15}\n\\]\nThe \\(W\\)-Rees space is a finite-type normal Gorenstein variety, with canonical generator of bidegree \\((r,n-1)\\), restricting to\n\\[\nc\\lambda^{-n}\\Omega_C\\wedge d\\lambda\n\\]\non \\(\\lambda\\neq0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Formula (6.13) follows directly from the basis. A vector \\(v^be_i\\) on the right has \\(b+T_i\\geq p\\); conversely, for such a vector choose \\(a=\\min(p,b)\\). This description is independent of compatible basis lifts and is multiplicative because the Taylor filtration is multiplicative.</p><p>More precisely, expand \\(e_ie_j\\) in the basis \\(v^ae_k\\). Compatibility with Taylor initials gives \\(T_k\\geq T_i+T_j\\) for every nonzero term. Equality of \\(W\\)-cost with \\(T_i+T_j\\) occurs exactly when \\(a=0\\) and \\(T_k=T_i+T_j\\); these are the products of the corresponding classes in \\(D\\). Factoring displayed \\(v\\)-powers proves\n\\[\n\\operatorname{gr}_WS=D[v].\n\\]\nIts Hilbert series follows from Proposition 11, and adjoining the variable of bidegree \\((m,1)\\) changes canonical degree \\((r-m,N)\\) to \\((r,n)\\).</p><p>Lemma 13.1 makes \\(G\\) reduced; it is Cohen–Macaulay and Gorenstein. The \\(W\\)-filtration has finite length in each original degree. Lifting finite generators of \\(G\\) gives a finite-type Rees algebra. Its total space is Cohen–Macaulay and Gorenstein. Away from \\(\\lambda=0\\) it is \\(C\\times\\mathbb G_m\\); a codimension-one central point is generic on a reduced central component, so its quotient by \\(\\lambda\\) is a field, and the total local ring is regular. Thus the total space is normal. Proposition 12 with \\(Q=n\\) gives the canonical formula.</p><p>For the quadratic-cone example in Section 6, take\n\\[\nS=\\mathbb C[a,z_1,\\ldots,z_N,b]/(ab-q(z)),\n\\qquad q(z)=\\sum_i z_i^2,\n\\]\nall of original degree one. Here \\(r=N\\), \\(m=1\\), and at \\([1:0:0]\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccc}\n&amp;a&amp;z_i&amp;b\\\\ \\hline\nT&amp;0&amp;1&amp;2\\\\\nW&amp;1&amp;1&amp;2.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nWith \\(v=a\\),\n\\[\nD=\\mathbb C[z_1,\\ldots,z_N,b]/(q(z)),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal X=\\{\\lambda ab-q(z)=0\\},\n\\]\nwith action weights \\((-1,1,1,2)\\). The central point \\(b=1\\), all other coordinates zero, has stabilizer \\(h=2\\), and the slice\n\\[\n\\{b=1\\}=\\{\\lambda a-q(z)=0\\}\n\\]\nis again an \\(n\\)-dimensional ordinary double point. Thus the original stabilizer \\(m\\) and the second stabilizer \\(h\\) are different.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "Reducedness of the quotient", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( D=\\bar S/(\\bar v) \\) is reduced.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition 13 before asserting reducedness of \\(D\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nD=\\bar S/(\\bar v)\n\\]\nis reduced.</p>", "proof": "<p>The \\(T\\)-Rees algebra is finite type by Proposition 11 and generator lifting. Its nonzero fibers are \\(S\\), and its central fiber is normal, Cohen–Macaulay and Gorenstein. The total space is Cohen–Macaulay and Gorenstein. It is normal: away from the central fiber it is \\(C\\times\\mathbb G_m\\); at a codimension-one central point, quotient by \\(\\lambda\\) is a field, so the total ring is regular. Thus \\(R_1+S_2\\) holds.</p><p>Let \\(H\\) be an irreducible component of \\(\\bar v=0\\), of multiplicity\n\\(\\nu=\\operatorname{ord}_H(\\bar v)\\geq1\\). At its generic point the central local ring is a DVR, and the total local ring is regular of dimension two. With parameters \\(\\lambda,t\\),\n\\[\nv=at^\\nu+\\lambda b\n\\]\nfor a unit \\(a\\). Take the monomial valuation\n\\[\nV(\\lambda)=1,\\qquad V(t)=1/\\nu.\n\\]\nIts total-space discrepancy is \\(1+1/\\nu\\). Its restriction \\(w\\) to \\(K(C)\\) satisfies\n\\[\nw(v)\\geq1,\\qquad w(e_i)\\geq T_i,\n\\qquad w(v^ae_i)\\geq a+T_i.\n\\]\nEvery nonconstant basis vector has positive lower cost, so \\(w\\) is centered exactly at \\(o\\).</p><p>The cost series is\n\\[\n\\sum_{a,i}z^{a+T_i}\n=(1-z)^{-1}\\operatorname{Hilb}_TD(z)\n=(1-z)^{-n}.\n\\]\nThe number of vectors of cost below \\(k\\) is \\(k^n/n!+O(k^{n-1})\\); they span the valuation quotient, hence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}(w)\\leq1.\n\\tag{6.11}\n\\]\nProposition 12 with \\(Q=N\\), \\(\\ell=1\\) gives\n\\[\nA_C(w)\\leq N+1/\\nu.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\nu\\geq2\\),\n\\[\n\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)\n\\leq(N+1/2)^{N+1}&lt;2N^{N+1},\n\\]\nsince\n\\[\n(N+1)\\log(1+1/(2N))\n&lt;\\frac{N+1}{2N}\\leq\\frac58&lt;\\log2.\n\\]\nThis contradicts the volume hypothesis. Every multiplicity is one, so \\(D\\) is generically reduced. It is Cohen–Macaulay, hence \\(S_1\\); the criterion \\(R_0+S_1\\) gives reducedness.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "A nontrivial second stabilizer", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a nonvertex closed point of \\(\\operatorname{Spec}D\\) whose grading stabilizer has order \\(h\\geq2\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition 13, in particular the singular cone \\(C\\), reduced ring \\(D\\), and its positive \\(T\\)-grading.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a nonvertex closed point of \\(\\operatorname{Spec}D\\) whose grading stabilizer has order \\(h\\geq2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>If all nonvertex stabilizers of \\(D\\) were trivial, Lemma 14.1 would make \\(D\\) polynomial on \\(N\\) degree-one generators. Proposition 13 would make \\(G=D[v]\\) polynomial on \\(n=N+1\\) generators.</p><p>Choose these generators homogeneous also in original degree and lift them to \\(S\\). Terminating cancellation in the \\(W\\)-filtration shows that the lifts generate \\(S\\). A surjection from a polynomial ring on \\(n\\) variables to the \\(n\\)-dimensional domain \\(S\\) has zero kernel. This would make \\(C\\) smooth, contrary to the standing assumption. Positive-grading stabilizers off the vertex are finite, so some has order at least two.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Free gradings and polynomial rings", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(E\\) is a polynomial ring on \\(N\\) generators of degree one.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\) be a finitely generated positively graded reduced equidimensional Cohen–Macaulay \\(\\mathbb C\\)-algebra of dimension \\(N\\geq2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(E_0=\\mathbb C\\) and\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Hilb}E(z)=(1-z)^{-N}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the grading action has trivial stabilizer at every nonvertex closed point.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(E\\) is a polynomial ring on \\(N\\) generators of degree one.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose positive homogeneous generators \\(a_j\\) of degrees \\(w_j\\). At a nonvertex closed point \\(q\\), the stabilizer is\n\\[\n\\mu_{\\gcd\\{w_j:a_j(q)\\neq0\\}}.\n\\]\nThe gcd is one. Inverting the product \\(f\\) of active generators, Bézout gives an invertible degree-one monomial \\(u=\\prod_j a_j^{c_j}\\), and\n\\[\nE_f=(E_f)_0[u,u^{-1}].\n\\tag{6.16}\n\\]\nThese opens cover the puncture because finite-type \\(\\mathbb C\\)-schemes are Jacobson. They make \\(L_Z=\\mathcal O_Z(1)\\), \\(Z=\\operatorname{Proj}E\\), an actual ample line bundle.</p><p>The scheme \\(Z\\) is reduced, equidimensional Cohen–Macaulay of dimension \\(N-1\\), and the Hilbert coefficient gives \\(L_Z^{N-1}=1\\). Each irreducible component has positive integral degree, so there is only one, and \\(Z\\) is integral. The image \\(V_0\\) of \\(E_1\\) in \\(H^0(Z,L_Z)\\) has dimension \\(N\\): restriction is injective because no nonzero homogeneous element of the reduced positive-dimensional equidimensional ring vanishes on the whole puncture.</p><p>Construct successive Cartier sections, tracking only the image of \\(V_0\\). Suppose an integral projective Cohen–Macaulay \\(Z_i\\) has image space \\(V_i\\) of dimension \\(N-i\\). Choose nonzero \\(s_i\\in V_i\\). Its zero scheme is nonempty by ampleness, pure and Cohen–Macaulay, and still has degree one. The component-degree formula gives one component of generic multiplicity one; \\(S_1\\) makes it reduced, hence integral. The restriction kernel is \\(\\mathbb Cs_i\\), since division by \\(s_i\\) gives a global regular function on proper integral \\(Z_i\\). Thus image dimension decreases by one.</p><p>After \\(N-1\\) cuts, the scheme is one reduced point and the remaining image has dimension one. Choose a final section nonzero there. The resulting \\(N\\) degree-one elements have no common zero on \\(\\operatorname{Proj}E\\), hence form a homogeneous system of parameters. Cohen–Macaulayness makes them a regular sequence, and the quotient Hilbert series is\n\\[\n(1-z)^N\\operatorname{Hilb}E(z)=1.\n\\]\nThe quotient is \\(\\mathbb C\\), so graded Nakayama gives generation by these elements. The polynomial-ring surjection is an isomorphism by the Hilbert series.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a singular normal Gorenstein boundary-zero klt algebraic germ \\(y\\in Y\\) of dimension \\(n\\), preserved by \\(\\mu_h\\), and a nonzero \\(\\lambda_Y\\in\\mathfrak m_{Y,y}\\) of character \\(-1\\), such that every …", "statement": "<p>**The same-dimensional singular klt slice: original Lemmas 7.1–7.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n=N+1\\geq5\\), and retain the singular cone \\(C\\) and large-stabilizer construction of Propositions 11–14, with\n  \\[\n  \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)\\geq2N^n.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal X=\\operatorname{Spec}\\operatorname{Rees}_WS,\n  \\qquad\n  G=D[v],\n  \\qquad\n  \\operatorname{Hilb}_WG(z)=(1-z)^{-n}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The Rees parameter \\(\\lambda\\) has character \\(-1\\); the action is trivial on \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(y_D\\neq0\\) in \\(\\operatorname{Spec}D\\) with stabilizer \\(\\mu_h\\), \\(h\\geq2\\), and let \\(y=(y_D,0)\\) in the central fiber.</p><p class=\"li\">The other family, \\(\\operatorname{Spec}\\bar S\\to\\mathbb A^1_t\\), has \\(t=\\bar v\\) of \\(T\\)-degree zero and\n  \\[\n  \\bar S[\\bar v^{-1}]\n  \\simeq\\mathbb C[\\bar v,\\bar v^{-1}][u_1,\\ldots,u_N],\n  \\qquad T(u_i)=1.\n  \\tag{7.2}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a singular normal Gorenstein boundary-zero klt algebraic germ \\(y\\in Y\\) of dimension \\(n\\), preserved by \\(\\mu_h\\), and a nonzero\n\\(\\lambda_Y\\in\\mathfrak m_{Y,y}\\) of character \\(-1\\), such that every quasi-monomial valuation \\(u\\) centered at \\(y\\) induces a quasi-monomial valuation \\(w\\) centered at \\(o\\). If \\(\\ell=u(\\lambda_Y)&gt;0\\), then\n\\[\nA_C(w)\\leq N\\ell+A_Y(u),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{vol}(w)\\leq\\ell^{-n}.\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 15.1 constructs the same-dimensional normal Gorenstein slice and its valuation comparison without assuming klt at the center. Lemma 15.2 proves it is klt, and Lemma 15.3 proves it is singular. These establish all assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "The uniformizing slice and its tests", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a normal Gorenstein \\(n\\)-dimensional germ \\(Y\\), preserved by \\(\\mu_h\\), with nonzero character-\\(-1\\) function \\(\\lambda_Y\\), klt away from \\(\\lambda_Y=0\\), and the valuation comparison (7.3).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition 15, but do not yet assume that the slice is klt or singular.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a normal Gorenstein \\(n\\)-dimensional germ \\(Y\\), preserved by \\(\\mu_h\\), with nonzero character-\\(-1\\) function \\(\\lambda_Y\\), klt away from \\(\\lambda_Y=0\\), and the valuation comparison (7.3). The comparison is valid before klt at \\(y\\) is established.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose positive-\\(W\\)-degree homogeneous \\(f\\) on \\(\\mathcal X\\) with \\(c=f(y)\\neq0\\), by lifting a nonvanishing homogeneous function at \\(y_D\\). Put \\(d=W(f)&gt;0\\), \\(U=\\{f\\neq0\\}\\), and \\(Y_0=\\{f=c\\}\\). The action map\n\\[\n\\theta:Y_0\\times\\mathbb G_m\\to U,\\qquad(q,a)\\mapsto a\\cdot q\n\\]\nis the finite étale cover adjoining \\(a^d=f/c\\). Let \\(Y\\) be the germ at \\(y\\). The étale product description gives normality, Gorensteinness and dimension \\(n\\). Since \\(h\\mid d\\), \\(\\mu_h\\) preserves the slice and fixes \\(y\\).</p><p>The restriction \\(\\lambda_Y\\) is nonzero and a nonzerodivisor, since its product-chart pullback differs from that of \\(\\lambda\\) by \\(a^{-1}\\). It vanishes at \\(y\\). Since \\(\\mathcal X_{\\lambda\\neq0}\\simeq C\\times\\mathbb G_m\\) is klt, \\(Y\\) is klt off \\(\\lambda_Y=0\\).</p><p>In \\(K(C)(\\lambda)\\),\n\\[\nf=\\lambda^{-d}s,\\qquad s\\in S,\n\\qquad \\lambda_Y^d=s/c.\n\\]\nAfter inverting \\(\\lambda_Y\\), each slice component is finite over \\(\\{s\\neq0\\}\\subset C\\) and dominates it: the equation is monic and the resulting algebra flat over \\(S[s^{-1}]\\). No component through \\(y\\) has \\(\\lambda_Y\\) identically zero. Thus \\(K(Y)/K(C)\\) is finite and \\(S\\) injects into \\(K(Y)\\).</p><p>Extend \\(u\\) to \\(K(Y)(a)\\) by the Gauss rule, with \\(a\\) of value zero and transcendental residue. Restrict through \\(\\theta\\) to a valuation \\(V\\) on \\(K(\\mathcal X)\\). Its center is generic on the orbit through \\(y\\), and the étale product gives\n\\[\nA_{\\mathcal X}(V)=A_Y(u),\n\\qquad V(\\lambda)=\\ell.\n\\tag{7.4}\n\\]\nNo \\(\\mu_h\\)-invariance of \\(u\\) is required.</p><p>For \\(w=V|_{K(C)}\\), invariance of \\(S\\) under the Rees action gives\n\\[\nw(s')=u(s'|_Y)\\qquad(s'\\in S).\n\\tag{7.5}\n\\]\nRestriction through the finite field extension preserves the Abhyankar equality, so \\(w\\) is quasi-monomial [18, Proposition 3.7].</p><p>If \\(s'\\in F^pS:=W_pS\\), the function \\(\\lambda^{-p}s'\\) is regular, hence\n\\[\nw(s')\\geq p\\ell.\n\\tag{7.6}\n\\]\nSince \\(F^1S\\) is the vertex maximal ideal, \\(w\\) is centered exactly at \\(o\\). Proposition 12 with \\(Q=n\\), together with (7.4), gives the discrepancy bound.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb C}S/F^pS=\\frac{p^n}{n!}+O(p^{n-1}).\n\\tag{7.7}\n\\]\nTaking \\(p=\\lceil k/\\ell\\rceil\\) gives \\(F^pS\\subset\\mathfrak a_k(w)\\). These quotients are supported at \\(o\\), so localization preserves lengths. Leading coefficients give \\(\\operatorname{vol}(w)\\leq\\ell^{-n}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.2", "orig": "The slice is klt", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The germ \\(y\\in Y\\) is klt.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Y,\\lambda_Y\\) be the germ and function of Lemma 15.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)\\geq2N^n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The germ \\(y\\in Y\\) is klt.</p>", "proof": "<p>Otherwise a divisor \\(E\\) on a log resolution has \\(A_Y(E)\\leq0\\) and center containing \\(y\\). Since \\(Y\\) is klt off \\(\\lambda_Y=0\\), its center lies there, and\n\\[\na=\\operatorname{ord}_E(\\lambda_Y)&gt;0.\n\\]\nResolve also \\(\\lambda_Y\\) and the ideal of \\(y\\), and choose a closed point \\(q\\in E\\) above \\(y\\). Give a local parameter defining \\(E\\) cost one and the remaining parameters positive costs tending to zero. The resulting quasi-monomial valuations \\(u_\\varepsilon\\), centered exactly at \\(y\\), satisfy\n\\[\nA_Y(u_\\varepsilon)=A_Y(E)+O(\\varepsilon),\n\\qquad\n\\ell_\\varepsilon=a+O(\\varepsilon).\n\\]\nTheir restrictions are centered at \\(o\\), where discrepancies are positive. Lemma 15.1 either gives an immediate contradiction through a nonpositive discrepancy upper bound, or gives\n\\[\n2N^n\n\\leq\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)\n\\leq\nA_C(w_\\varepsilon)^n\\operatorname{vol}(w_\\varepsilon)\n\\leq\n\\left(N+\\frac{A_Y(u_\\varepsilon)}{\\ell_\\varepsilon}\\right)^n.\n\\]\nThe last base is positive when the chain applies, with limit superior at most \\(N\\). Let \\(\\varepsilon\\to0\\), contradicting \\(2N^n&gt;N^n\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.3", "orig": "Singularity of the slice", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The germ \\(y\\in Y\\) is singular.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the slice of Lemma 15.1 and the equivariant other family (7.2).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(h\\geq2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The germ \\(y\\in Y\\) is singular.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(Y\\) smooth. The étale product implies \\(\\mathcal X\\) smooth at the corresponding point. In its regular local ring, \\(\\lambda\\) has character \\(-1\\). Lift the polynomial parameter \\(v\\in G=D[v]\\) equivariantly to \\(\\widetilde v\\) of character \\(1\\). Its differential is nonzero because the class of \\(v\\) in the central cotangent space is nonzero.</p><p>Thus \\(H=\\{\\widetilde v=0\\}\\) is smooth, and \\(D\\) at \\(y_D\\) is cut out in \\(H\\) by the nonzero equation \\(\\lambda|_H\\). Nonzeroness follows because \\(\\lambda,\\widetilde v\\) form a regular sequence. If this equation has a linear part, \\(D\\) is smooth; otherwise its minimal hypersurface relation has the nontrivial character \\(-1\\bmod h\\).</p><p>Complete the other family \\(\\bar S\\) at \\(y_D\\), flat over \\(\\mathbb C[[t]]\\), where \\(t=\\bar v\\) is invariant. Lift a minimal eigen-coordinate system of \\(D\\) equivariantly. Complete Nakayama gives\n\\[\nP=\\mathbb C[[t,x_1,\\ldots,x_e]]\n\\twoheadrightarrow\n\\widehat{\\mathcal O}_{\\operatorname{Spec}\\bar S,y_D}\n\\]\nwith kernel \\(I\\), and flatness identifies \\(I/tI\\) with the special-fiber kernel.</p><p>If \\(D\\) is smooth, Nakayama gives \\(I=0\\). Otherwise lift its minimal character-\\(-1\\) relation to \\(I\\), average to that character, and obtain \\(I=(\\widetilde F)\\). Invariance of \\(t\\) and nontriviality of the character imply\n\\[\n\\widetilde F(t,0,\\ldots,0)=0.\n\\]\nIn both cases \\(x_1=\\cdots=x_e=0\\) gives a \\(\\mu_h\\)-fixed formal section.</p><p>A positive-\\(T\\)-degree function nonzero at \\(y_D\\) lifts homogeneously to \\(\\bar S\\) and takes a unit value on this section, remaining nonzero over \\(\\mathbb C((t))\\). But (7.2) identifies the generic fiber, with its action, with affine \\(N\\)-space whose coordinates all have character \\(1\\). For \\(h\\geq2\\), its fixed-point scheme is the origin, where every positive-\\(T\\)-degree function vanishes. Contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "The refined slice estimate", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\operatorname{vol}(w)\\leq \\min_{0\\leq x\\leq1} \\left\\{\\frac{x^n}{\\ell^n} +(1-x)^n\\frac{\\sigma}{h}\\right\\}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the cone and singular klt slice of Proposition 15.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u_*\\) be a minimizing valuation at \\(y\\in Y\\). It is quasi-monomial and \\(\\mu_h\\)-invariant by existence and uniqueness up to scaling.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  A'=A_Y(u_*),\\qquad\n  \\ell=u_*(\\lambda_Y)&gt;0,\\qquad\n  \\sigma=\\operatorname{vol}(u_*)&gt;0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(w\\) be its induced valuation on \\(C\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}(w)\\leq\n\\min_{0\\leq x\\leq1}\n\\left\\{\\frac{x^n}{\\ell^n}\n+(1-x)^n\\frac{\\sigma}{h}\\right\\}.\n\\tag{7.8}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathfrak a_k=\\{s\\in S:w(s)\\geq k\\}\\). For \\(p&gt;0\\), split colength exactly:\n\\[\n\\dim S/\\mathfrak a_k\n=\n\\dim S/(F^pS+\\mathfrak a_k)\n+\n\\dim F^pS/(F^pS\\cap\\mathfrak a_k).\n\\tag{7.9}\n\\]\nThe first term is at most \\(\\dim S/F^pS\\).</p><p>For the second, restriction and division give the regular map\n\\[\nF^pS\\to R,\\qquad\ns\\mapsto\\lambda_Y^{-p}(s|_Y).\n\\]\nIts image has character \\(p\\bmod h\\), because \\(s\\) is invariant and \\(\\lambda_Y\\) has character \\(-1\\). Formula (7.5) gives an injection\n\\[\nF^pS/(F^pS\\cap\\mathfrak a_k)\n\\hookrightarrow\n(R/\\mathfrak a_{k-p\\ell})_{p\\bmod h}.\n\\tag{7.10}\n\\]\nThis compares one character quotient, not the full upstairs length.</p><p>For \\(0&lt;x&lt;1\\), take \\(p=\\lfloor kx/\\ell\\rfloor\\). Apply the Hilbert asymptotic (7.7) from Proposition 15 and Lemma 16.1. Since there are finitely many characters for the fixed slice, errors are uniform as \\(p\\bmod h\\) varies. Multiply by \\(n!/k^n\\) and pass to the limit:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}(w)\n\\leq x^n/\\ell^n+(1-x)^n\\sigma/h.\n\\]\nContinuity includes endpoints; minimize over \\(x\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.1", "orig": "Equal leading character lengths", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For each \\(\\chi\\in\\mathbb Z/h\\mathbb Z\\), \\( \\dim_{\\mathbb C}(R/\\mathfrak a_t)_\\chi = \\frac{\\sigma}{h\\,n!}t^n+o(t^n). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(R=\\mathcal O_{Y,y}\\), and let\n  \\(\\mathfrak a_t=\\{g\\in R:u_*(g)\\geq t\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(\\mu_h\\)-invariance, \\(\\sigma\\), and the character-\\(-1\\) function \\(\\lambda_Y\\) of value \\(\\ell\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each \\(\\chi\\in\\mathbb Z/h\\mathbb Z\\),\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb C}(R/\\mathfrak a_t)_\\chi\n=\n\\frac{\\sigma}{h\\,n!}t^n+o(t^n).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Multiplication by \\(\\lambda_Y^j\\) gives an injection\n\\[\n(R/\\mathfrak a_{t-j\\ell})_\\chi\n\\hookrightarrow\n(R/\\mathfrak a_t)_{\\chi-j}.\n\\]\nIts kernel is exactly the indicated cutoff, by additivity of valuation on products. Any two characters are connected with \\(0\\leq j\\leq h-1\\). With \\(H_0=(h-1)\\ell\\), summing the resulting comparisons gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}(R/\\mathfrak a_{t-H_0})\n\\leq\nh\\dim_{\\mathbb C}(R/\\mathfrak a_t)_\\chi\n\\leq\n\\operatorname{length}(R/\\mathfrak a_{t+H_0}).\n\\]\nThe total length is \\(\\sigma t^n/n!+o(t^n)\\). The argument uses only the primitive character of the nonzero function \\(\\lambda_Y\\), not the tangent representation.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "Optimization", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( d(o,C)\\leq \\frac{\\bigl((N/n)\\ell+A'/n\\bigr)^n} {\\bigl(\\ell^{n/N}+(h/\\sigma)^{1/N}\\bigr)^N} \\) and \\( d(o,C) \\leq (N/n)^n+(A'/n)^n\\sigma/h \\leq\\frac{p_n}{2}+\\frac{M_n}{2}. \\) Equality in the Hölder inequality between …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition 16.</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(M_n\\) to be the supremum of \\(d(x,X)\\) over all singular boundary-zero complex algebraic klt \\(n\\)-fold germs with \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier canonical divisor.</p><p class=\"li\">Only \\(M_n\\leq1\\) and its defining supremum property are assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">Recall \\(p_n=2(N/n)^n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nd(o,C)\\leq\n\\frac{\\bigl((N/n)\\ell+A'/n\\bigr)^n}\n{\\bigl(\\ell^{n/N}+(h/\\sigma)^{1/N}\\bigr)^N}\n\\tag{7.11}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nd(o,C)\n\\leq\n(N/n)^n+(A'/n)^n\\sigma/h\n\\leq\\frac{p_n}{2}+\\frac{M_n}{2}.\n\\tag{7.12}\n\\]\nEquality in the Hölder inequality between these bounds holds precisely when\n\\[\n(A')^N\\ell\\,\\frac{\\sigma}{h}=N^N.\n\\tag{7.13}\n\\]\nThe unique optimizer in the colength estimate is\n\\[\nx=\\frac{\\ell^{n/N}}\n{\\ell^{n/N}+(h/\\sigma)^{1/N}}.\n\\tag{7.14}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Direct differentiation gives\n\\[\n\\min_{0\\leq x\\leq1}\n\\{\\ell^{-n}x^n+(\\sigma/h)(1-x)^n\\}\n=\n\\bigl(\\ell^{n/N}+(h/\\sigma)^{1/N}\\bigr)^{-N},\n\\]\nwith optimizer (7.14). Combine Proposition 16 with\n\\(A_C(w)\\leq N\\ell+A'\\) to obtain (7.11).</p><p>Apply Hölder with conjugate exponents \\(n/N\\) and \\(n\\) to\n\\[\n(\\ell,(h/\\sigma)^{1/n}),\n\\qquad\n(N,A'(\\sigma/h)^{1/n}).\n\\]\nTheir scalar product is \\(N\\ell+A'\\), so\n\\[\n(N\\ell+A')^n\n\\leq\n\\bigl(\\ell^{n/N}+(h/\\sigma)^{1/N}\\bigr)^N\n\\bigl(N^n+(A')^n\\sigma/h\\bigr).\n\\]\nIts equality condition is (7.13).</p><p>Proposition 15 makes \\(Y\\) a singular boundary-zero klt germ of the same dimension \\(n\\). Since \\(u_*\\) computes its minimum,\n\\[\n(A'/n)^n\\sigma=d(y,Y)\\leq M_n.\n\\]\nUse \\(h\\geq2\\) and \\((N/n)^n=p_n/2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 18", "orig": "The upper bound", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( M_n\\leq p_n. \\) Equivalently, every singular boundary-zero klt \\(n\\)-dimensional germ satisfies \\( \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\\leq2(n-1)^n. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n=N+1\\geq5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the local ODP theorem in dimensions smaller than \\(n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M_n\\) and \\(p_n\\) be as in Proposition 17.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nM_n\\leq p_n.\n\\]\nEquivalently, every singular boundary-zero klt \\(n\\)-dimensional germ satisfies\n\\[\n\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\\leq2(n-1)^n.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(M_n&gt;p_n\\). Choose singular klt germs with densities \\(d_i&gt;p_n\\) tending to \\(M_n\\). Proposition 2 gives singular Gorenstein K-polystable cones \\(C_i\\), smooth off their vertices, of the same densities.</p><p>Fix \\(i\\) and choose the fast gradings of Proposition 3 on \\(C_i\\). If \\(m_{\\max}\\leq r/N\\) holds along an infinite subsequence, Proposition 4 makes \\(C_i\\) smooth or the standard ODP cone. Smoothness contradicts singularity; the ODP possibility contradicts \\(d_i&gt;p_n\\), by Proposition 1.</p><p>Thus the large-stabilizer condition holds for sufficiently far terms. Propositions 5–17 apply to one such term and yield\n\\[\nd_i\\leq p_n/2+M_n/2.\n\\]\nLetting \\(i\\to\\infty\\) gives \\(M_n\\leq p_n/2+M_n/2\\), contradicting \\(M_n&gt;p_n\\).</p><p>All sufficiently fast choices are made separately on each fixed cone. No attained supremum and no approximation uniform over different cones are required. The same-dimensional slice enters only through the defining supremum \\(M_n\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 19", "orig": "The equality cone", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(C\\) is the standard ordinary double point cone.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n=N+1\\geq5\\), and assume the lower-dimensional local theorem.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C\\) be a singular cone supplied by Proposition 2, with\n  \\[\n  d(o,C)=p_n.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The same-dimensional upper bound of Proposition 18 is now available.</p><p class=\"li\">Use fast gradings from Proposition 3, and, in the large-stabilizer range, choose the original point of maximal stabilizer \\(m=m_{\\max}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(C\\) is the standard ordinary double point cone.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the fast primitive gradings of Proposition 3. A term either satisfies \\(Nm_{\\max}\\leq r\\) already, or lies in the large-stabilizer range. For sufficiently far terms in the latter range, Lemmas 19.1–19.3 again give \\(Nm_{\\max}\\leq r\\).</p><p>Thus the entire sequence eventually satisfies the small-stabilizer hypothesis. Proposition 4 makes \\(C\\) smooth or the standard quadratic cone. Since its vertex is singular, only the ordinary double point remains.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 19.1", "orig": "Consequences of equality", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( h=2,\\qquad d(y,Y)=p_n,\\qquad A'=N\\ell. \\) After rescaling \\(u_*\\) to make \\(\\ell=1\\), \\( A'=N,\\qquad \\sigma=2,\\qquad A_C(w)=2N,\\qquad \\operatorname{vol}(w)=2^{-N}, \\qquad w=\\frac{2N}{n}\\operatorname{wt}_\\xi. \\) The …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition 19 and make the sufficiently fast large-stabilizer construction.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Y,u_*,A',\\ell,\\sigma,w,h\\) be as in Propositions 15–17.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nh=2,\\qquad d(y,Y)=p_n,\\qquad A'=N\\ell.\n\\]\nAfter rescaling \\(u_*\\) to make \\(\\ell=1\\),\n\\[\nA'=N,\\qquad \\sigma=2,\\qquad\nA_C(w)=2N,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{vol}(w)=2^{-N},\n\\qquad\nw=\\frac{2N}{n}\\operatorname{wt}_\\xi.\n\\tag{7.15}\n\\]\nThe optimal splitting fraction is \\(x=1/2\\), and all inequalities producing the refined estimate are equalities in leading coefficients.</p>", "proof": "<p>The left endpoint of (7.11)–(7.12) is \\(p_n\\), and Proposition 18 makes the right endpoint at most \\(p_n\\). Every inequality is an equality, so\n\\[\nh=2,\\qquad (A')^n\\sigma=2N^n.\n\\]\nTogether with (7.13),\n\\[\nN^N=(A')^N\\ell\\sigma/2=N^n\\ell/A',\n\\]\nhence \\(A'=N\\ell\\).</p><p>Rescale by \\(\\ell^{-1}\\); then \\(A'=N\\), \\(\\sigma=2\\), and (7.14) gives \\(x=1/2\\). The volume and discrepancy bounds become\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}(w)\\leq2^{-N},\n\\qquad\nA_C(w)\\leq2N.\n\\]\nBut\n\\[\n2N^n=\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)\n\\leq A_C(w)^n\\operatorname{vol}(w)\n\\leq(2N)^n2^{-N}=2N^n.\n\\]\nBoth upper bounds are attained. Thus \\(w\\) minimizes normalized volume, and uniqueness up to scaling, with \\(A_C(\\operatorname{wt}_\\xi)=n\\), gives the last identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 19.2", "orig": "The distinguished section cannot have value greater than two", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( w(v)\\leq2. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the normalization \\(\\ell=1\\) of Lemma 19.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v\\in S_m\\) be the section defining the two filtrations.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nw(v)\\leq2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(w(v)&gt;2\\). Choose fixed real \\(t\\) with\n\\[\n1/w(v)&lt;t&lt;1/2,\n\\]\nand put\n\\[\na_k=\\lceil tk\\rceil,\\qquad p_k=\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor.\n\\]\nLet \\(E_k\\) be the span of the basis vectors \\(v^be_i\\) of \\(W\\)-level less than \\(p_k-a_k\\).</p><p>Multiplication by \\(v^{a_k}\\) injects \\(E_k\\) into \\(S/F^{p_k}S\\), because the initial of \\(v\\) is a regular polynomial variable in \\(G=D[v]\\), so raises every nonzero initial level exactly by \\(a_k\\). Every element of \\(v^{a_k}E_k\\) has \\(w\\)-value greater than \\(k\\), since \\(w\\) is nonnegative on \\(S\\) and \\(a_kw(v)&gt;k\\).</p><p>Thus the kernel of\n\\[\nS/F^{p_k}S\\to S/(F^{p_k}S+\\mathfrak a_k(w))\n\\]\nhas dimension at least\n\\[\n\\dim E_k\n=\\dim S/F^{p_k-a_k}S\n=\\frac{(1/2-t)^n}{n!}k^n+o(k^n).\n\\]\nThis is a strict positive leading loss in the first term of the splitting (7.9). The second still satisfies the character bound. At \\(x=1/2\\), \\(\\ell=1\\) and \\(\\sigma/h=1\\), this gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}(w)\n\\leq2^{-n}+2^{-n}-(1/2-t)^n\n&lt;2^{-N},\n\\]\ncontradicting Lemma 19.1. Only one exponent \\(a_k\\) is counted at each cutoff, so there is no overlap of collections of powers.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 19.3", "orig": "Rounding the maximal stabilizer", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( Nm_{\\max}\\leq r \\) for every sufficiently far such term.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the fixed equality cone and fast gradings of Proposition 19.</p><p class=\"li\">For sufficiently far terms in the large-stabilizer range, use the maximal original stabilizer \\(m=m_{\\max}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nNm_{\\max}\\leq r\n\\]\nfor every sufficiently far such term.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix torus eigen-generators \\(g_j\\) of characters \\(\\chi_j\\), and write\n\\[\n\\zeta=(r/n)\\xi+\\epsilon,\\qquad\\|\\epsilon\\|\\to0.\n\\]\nTheir \\(\\zeta\\)-degrees lie between \\(c_1r\\) and \\(c_2r\\), while\n\\(m\\leq\\max_j\\deg_\\zeta g_j=O(r)\\).</p><p>The section \\(v\\in S_m\\) has a \\(\\zeta\\)-homogeneous polynomial lift. Each monomial uses a bounded number of generators, so its possible torus characters lie in a fixed finite set. For each occurring character \\(\\chi\\),\n\\[\nm=\\frac rn\\langle\\xi,\\chi\\rangle+\\langle\\epsilon,\\chi\\rangle,\n\\]\nhence uniformly\n\\[\n\\langle\\xi,\\chi\\rangle\n=\\frac{nm}{r}+O(\\|\\epsilon\\|/r).\n\\]\nThe minimum over nonzero components is \\(\\operatorname{wt}_\\xi(v)\\). Lemma 19.1 gives\n\\[\nw(v)=\\frac{2Nm}{r}+O(\\|\\epsilon\\|/r)\n=\\frac{2Nm}{r}+o(1/r).\n\\tag{7.16}\n\\]\nLemma 19.2 implies\n\\[\nNm-r\\leq O(\\|\\epsilon\\|)=o(1).\n\\]\nThe left side is integral, so is nonpositive for sufficiently far terms. On an exact rational grading the error is zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 20", "orig": "Hypersurface equality", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\\leq2(n-1)^n. \\) If equality holds, the quadratic part of \\(F\\) is nondegenerate, and the analytic germ is an ordinary double point.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(x\\in X\\) be a singular klt hypersurface germ of dimension \\(n\\geq2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\) be a local equation in regular ambient parameters \\(z_0,\\ldots,z_n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\\leq2(n-1)^n.\n\\]\nIf equality holds, the quadratic part of \\(F\\) is nondegenerate, and the analytic germ is an ordinary double point.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(q=\\operatorname{mult}_x(F)\\geq2\\). Equal weights in Lemma 20.1 give\n\\[\n\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\\leq q(n+1-q)^n,\n\\]\nand klt implies \\(q&lt;n+1\\). For\n\\(b(t)=t(n+1-t)^n\\),\n\\[\nb'(t)=(n+1)(1-t)(n+1-t)^{n-1}&lt;0\n\\qquad(1&lt;t&lt;n+1).\n\\]\nThus \\(q&gt;2\\) gives a strict bound below \\(2(n-1)^n\\).</p><p>If \\(q=2\\) and the quadratic part is degenerate, choose after a linear change a direction absent from it. Give that direction weight \\(1-\\varepsilon\\), all others weight one, where rational \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;1/3\\). Every term of ordinary degree at least three has weighted degree greater than two, so \\(d_F=2\\). Lemma 20.1 gives\n\\[\n\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\n\\leq\\frac{2(n-1-\\varepsilon)^n}{1-\\varepsilon}.\n\\]\nIts logarithmic derivative at zero is\n\\[\n-\\frac n{n-1}+1=-\\frac1{n-1}&lt;0.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently small \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) this is strictly below \\(2(n-1)^n\\). Equality therefore forces a nondegenerate quadratic part, and the holomorphic Morse lemma gives the analytic ODP.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 20.1", "orig": "A hypersurface test", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X) \\leq \\frac{d_F\\left(\\sum_{i=0}^nw_i-d_F\\right)^n} {\\prod_{i=0}^nw_i}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(x\\in X\\) be a klt hypersurface germ of dimension \\(n\\), with equation \\(F\\) in regular ambient parameters \\(z_0,\\ldots,z_n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(w_0,\\ldots,w_n\\) be positive rational weights.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d_F\\) be the weighted order of \\(F\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\n\\leq\n\\frac{d_F\\left(\\sum_{i=0}^nw_i-d_F\\right)^n}\n{\\prod_{i=0}^nw_i}.\n\\tag{8.1}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Common rescaling preserves both sides, so assume integral weights. Choose a common positive multiple \\(D_1\\) of the weights, and put\n\\[\nI=(z_i^{D_1/w_i}:0\\leq i\\leq n)\\subset\\mathcal O_{X,x}.\n\\]\nIt is primary.</p><p>The ambient weighted valuation has discrepancy \\(\\sum_iw_i\\), value \\(d_F\\) on \\(F\\), and value \\(D_1\\) on the ambient ideal. Hypersurface inversion of adjunction [11, Theorem 1.1] gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{lct}_X(I)\n\\leq\\frac{\\sum_iw_i-d_F}{D_1}.\n\\tag{8.2}\n\\]\nThe numerator is positive because \\(X\\) is klt and \\(I\\) is primary.</p><p>Let \\(J_t\\) be the quotient weighted-order filtration. The monomial floor inequality gives\n\\[\nJ_{(k+n+1)D_1}\\subset I^k\\subset J_{kD_1}.\n\\]\nIndeed, weight at least \\((k+n+1)D_1\\) forces the sum of the floors\n\\(\\lfloor\\alpha_iw_i/D_1\\rfloor\\) to be at least \\(k\\).</p><p>The associated graded quotient is\n\\[\n\\mathbb C[z_0,\\ldots,z_n]/(\\operatorname{in}_wF),\n\\]\nwith Hilbert series\n\\[\n\\frac{1-t^{d_F}}{\\prod_i(1-t^{w_i})},\n\\]\nwhether the initial equation is reducible or nonreduced. The inclusions and leading coefficient give\n\\[\ne(I)=\\frac{D_1^nd_F}{\\prod_iw_i}.\n\\tag{8.3}\n\\]</p><p>Choose a divisor \\(E\\) computing \\(\\operatorname{lct}_X(I)\\), and put \\(q=\\operatorname{ord}_E(I)&gt;0\\). Its center is \\(x\\). Since\n\\[\nI^k\\subset\\mathfrak a_{kq}(E),\n\\]\none has \\(q^n\\operatorname{vol}(E)\\leq e(I)\\). Testing normalized volume gives\n\\[\n\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\n\\leq A_X(E)^n\\operatorname{vol}(E)\n\\leq\\operatorname{lct}_X(I)^ne(I).\n\\]\nSubstitute (8.2)–(8.3).</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 0, "cite"], [2, 40, "cite"], [3, 0, "cite"], [3, 6, "cite"], [3, 15, "cite"], [3, 40, "cite"], [3, 41, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [6, 9, "cite"], [6, 18, "cite"], [6, 40, "cite"], [6, 41, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [8, 10, "cite"], [9, 40, "cite"], [9, 41, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [11, 16, "cite"], [12, 9, "cite"], [12, 14, "cite"], [12, 16, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 9, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [15, 17, "cite"], [15, 23, "cite"], [15, 24, "cite"], [15, 26, "cite"], [15, 40, "cite"], [16, 23, "cite"], [16, 40, "cite"], [17, 16, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [18, 16, "cite"], [19, 23, "cite"], [19, 40, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [20, 22, "cite"], [20, 33, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [21, 33, "cite"], [22, 19, "cite"], [22, 33, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [23, 26, "cite"], [23, 40, "cite"], [24, 26, "cite"], [24, 40, "cite"], [25, 24, "cite"], [26, 27, "cite"], [26, 40, "cite"], [27, 29, "cite"], [27, 30, "cite"], [27, 33, "cite"], [27, 40, "cite"], [28, 29, "cite"], [28, 30, "cite"], [28, 33, "cite"], [28, 34, "cite"], [28, 40, "cite"], [29, 31, "cite"], [29, 33, "cite"], [29, 40, "cite"], [30, 29, "cite"], [31, 33, "cite"], [31, 40, "cite"], [32, 31, "cite"], [33, 37, "cite"], [33, 39, "cite"], [33, 40, "cite"], [33, 42, "cite"], [34, 33, "cite"], [34, 35, "cite"], [34, 36, "cite"], [35, 33, "cite"], [36, 33, "cite"], [37, 39, "cite"], [37, 40, "cite"], [37, 42, "cite"], [38, 37, "cite"], [39, 40, "cite"], [39, 42, "cite"], [40, 0, "cite"], [40, 41, "cite"], [40, 42, "cite"], [41, 0, "cite"], [42, 41, "cite"], [42, 43, "cite"], [42, 44, "cite"], [43, 41, "cite"], [43, 44, "cite"], [44, 41, "cite"], [45, 0, "cite"], [46, 45, "cite"]]});
